-- transaction and is safe to re-run.

BEGIN;

UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the added H⁺ from ketoacids shifts the bicarbonate equilibrium (H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ CO₂ + H₂O) to the right, and the elevated H⁺/CO₂ stimulates chemoreceptors to increase ventilation; blowing off CO₂ pulls the equilibrium further to the right, consuming H⁺ and raising pH back toward normal. (Choice D) is correct: increased ventilation removes CO₂, which by Le Châtelier drives the buffer toward CO₂ and consumes the excess H⁺, partially correcting the low pH. (Choice A) is incorrect: decreasing ventilation retains CO₂, which shifts the equilibrium toward more H₂CO₃ and more H⁺, worsening the acidosis rather than compensating; slower breathing is the response to alkalosis, not acidosis. (Choice B) is incorrect: it pairs the correct direction (increased ventilation) with a contradictory mechanism. Increased ventilation removes CO₂, it does not retain it, and adding CO₂ would generate more H⁺, not neutralize it. (Choice C) is incorrect: decreased ventilation is the wrong direction for an acid load, and H⁺ ions are not themselves exhaled; the lungs excrete acid only indirectly as CO₂ gas. This question tests the skill of reasoning from a described acid-base disturbance to the direction and mechanism of respiratory compensation.$px$ WHERE id = 'e1d47295-2a39-4e04-94bf-4883267b046f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because the two components of the mucociliary escalator have distinct jobs: the sticky mucus layer (secreted by goblet cells) physically traps inhaled microbes and particles, while the coordinated beating of the cilia provides the transport that propels that mucus, along with its trapped load, upward toward the pharynx to be swallowed or expelled. With mucus production intact but ciliary beating weak and uncoordinated, bacteria are still caught in the secretions, but the transport step fails, so the contaminated mucus stagnates and accumulates in the lower airway, predisposing to infection. (Choice C) is correct: trapping (a mucus function) is preserved, but clearance (a ciliary function) is impaired, so trapped material builds up instead of being removed. (Choice A) is incorrect: trapping is a property of the sticky mucus layer, not of the cilia; microbes do not pass freely simply because cilia are dysfunctional. (Choice B) is incorrect: it wrongly assigns both trapping and transport to the cilia; trapping is the role of the mucus, so catching still occurs. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reverses the roles by claiming the mucus layer drives its own upward transport; the mucus is passive and relies on ciliary beating for movement, so clearance is not normal here. This question requires the skill of reasoning about how the distinct functions of two structures combine to accomplish a physiological process.$px$ WHERE id = 'e1fd5246-fda7-4991-803c-e2335dcb3638';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because the bend arises only when the sliding force dynein generates between doublets is opposed by structural constraints, so the shear is redirected into curvature rather than lengthwise displacement. The flagellum and cilium share a 9+2 array: the text states that "a single flagellum or cilium is made of a ring of nine microtubule doublets, surrounding a single microtubule doublet in the center," and microtubules "provide a track along which vesicles move," which are the same tracks that motor proteins like dynein walk along. Dynein arms anchored on one doublet step toward the minus end of the neighboring doublet, which by itself would simply make adjacent doublets slide past one another. Because the nexin links cross-tie the doublets and the basal body anchors the base, free sliding is prevented; the resisted shear instead forces the axoneme to bend locally, and waves of such bending propagate down the flagellum to generate forward thrust (cellular locomotion, the listed function of flagella). (Choice A) The central pair does not elongate by ATP-driven lengthening, and axonemal motion is bending, not telescopic elongation. This option misattributes both the mechanism and the resulting motion. (Choice B) Correct: the structural resistance (nexin links plus the basal anchor) converts interdoublet sliding into bending, which is the propulsive stroke. (Choice C) If dynein let the doublets slide freely, the axoneme would mostly telescope/elongate rather than bend; the whole point of the nexin links is that sliding is constrained, so unconstrained free sliding cannot produce the bend. (Choice D) Dynein does not shorten an individual microtubule the way myosin pulls on actin in a sarcomere; it walks along an adjacent doublet to create sliding between two filaments, not contraction within one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the axoneme's structure with the behavior of its motor and anchoring proteins to predict how a sliding force is transformed into a bending stroke.$px$ WHERE id = 'e2712342-54f4-4f56-8dce-6db771186790';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because sperm flagella and respiratory cilia are built from the identical microtubule scaffold (the "9 + 2 array"), and both are bent by the same dynein motor, so one dynein defect disables both. The text states that "flagella and cilia share a common structural arrangement of microtubules called a '9 + 2 array.' ... a single flagellum or cilium is made of a ring of nine microtubule doublets, surrounding a single microtubule doublet in the center." Because a sperm moves by its flagellum and the respiratory tract is cleared by cilia that "trap particulate matter and move it toward your nostrils," and both organelles depend on the dynein arms to slide adjacent doublets past one another to generate the bending stroke, a single inherited loss of those dynein arms predicts simultaneous immotile sperm and failed mucus clearance. (Choice A) Microfilaments power movement through actin and its motor "myosin," which drives muscle contraction and cytoplasmic streaming, not the beating of cilia and flagella, which are microtubule-based. This misassigns the motor system. (Choice B) Cilia and flagella share a "9 + 2 array," not a "9 + 3" array. The "9 + 2" label is correct while "9 + 3" is a fabricated arrangement, so the structural premise is false even though the shared-defect logic sounds right. (Choice C) is correct because both organelles are the same dynein-driven 9 + 2 axoneme, so a single dynein-arm defect leaves neither able to beat. One lesion explains both the immotile sperm and the poor mucus clearance. (Choice D) Microvilli are short actin-based surface projections that increase surface area for absorption; they neither beat nor propel fluid and are not built on the dynein-driven microtubule axoneme, so they cannot explain immotile sperm. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the shared 9 + 2 axoneme-and-dynein structure to predict why one molecular defect produces two seemingly unrelated clinical findings.$px$, options = $px$[{"text":"The defect disrupts the actin-myosin machinery that powers both the sperm tail and the respiratory cilia, halting their movement.","label":"A"},{"text":"The defect collapses the \"9 + 3\" microtubule array unique to motile cilia and flagella, so only ciliated and flagellated cells lose motility.","label":"B"},{"text":"Sperm flagella and respiratory cilia share the same dynein-powered \"9 + 2\" axoneme, so removing the dynein arms abolishes the microtubule sliding that bends both structures.","label":"C"},{"text":"The defect shortens the apical microvilli of respiratory and reproductive epithelia, eliminating the surface projections that sweep fluid across the cell.","label":"D"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'e2d7e2c8-2d91-4e4d-bef8-fd5428dfc3d6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.' The number of orbitals in a subshell is \(2l + 1\), and the number of radial nodes in an orbital is \(n - l - 1\); together these let you recover a specific orbital's quantum numbers from measurable features. Five orbitals in the subshell give \(2l + 1 = 5\), so \(l = 2\) (a \(d\) subshell), and one radial node gives \(n - l - 1 = 1\), so \(n = l + 2 = 4\), which is a \(4d\) orbital. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(n = 2\) would follow from misreading the single radial node as the total node count and setting \(n - 1 = 1\), which ignores the angular contribution and the \(l = 2\) requirement (a \(2d\) orbital does not even exist, since \(l\) must be less than \(n\)). (Choice B) is incorrect: \(n = 3\) is the smallest shell that contains a \(d\) subshell, so it is tempting once you identify \(l = 2\), but a \(3d\) orbital has \(n - l - 1 = 3 - 2 - 1 = 0\) radial nodes, contradicting the observed single node. (Choice C) is correct: combining \(l = 2\) from the five-orbital subshell with one radial node gives \(n - 2 - 1 = 1\), so \(n = 4\). (Choice D) is incorrect: \(n = 5\) comes from over-mapping the "five orbitals" clue directly onto \(n\), but the count of five orbitals fixes \(l\), not \(n\); a \(5d\) orbital would have \(5 - 2 - 1 = 2\) radial nodes, not one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationships \(2l + 1\) and \(n - l - 1\) to two independent measured features to deduce \(n\).$px$ WHERE id = 'e2f1ef53-dd7b-49f5-9dbf-eee878059b3e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is C because an inhibitory postsynaptic potential hyperpolarizes the membrane, which drives the membrane potential away from threshold and therefore reduces the probability that the neuron will reach threshold and fire. The Anatomy & Physiology text states that "Hyperpolarization in a postsynaptic potential is an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) because it causes the membrane potential to move away from threshold," and the Biology text explains that when GABA is released it "binds to and opens Cl- channels. Cl- ions enter the cell and hyperpolarizes the membrane, making the neuron less likely to fire an action potential." Because reaching threshold (e.g., depolarizing from -70 mV toward -55 mV) is what triggers an action potential, any input that moves Vm in the opposite, more-negative direction makes that crossing harder to achieve. (Choice A) is wrong because hyperpolarization moves Vm away from threshold, not toward it, so it cannot push a near-threshold cell over the threshold or trigger firing. (Choice B) is wrong because an IPSP hyperpolarizes rather than depolarizes the membrane; inhibition is not simply a slower form of excitation, so it does not produce a merely delayed action potential. (Choice C) is correct because the IPSP-driven hyperpolarization moves Vm farther from threshold and lowers firing probability. (Choice D) is wrong because action potentials are "all or none." A stronger or weaker stimulus does not make a "bigger" or smaller action potential, and all action potentials peak at the same voltage, so an IPSP cannot reduce action-potential amplitude. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the functional consequence (firing probability) of an ionic event (Cl- influx and hyperpolarization) on a neuron poised near threshold, rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'e33103ed-3d7a-4d75-9efd-7cdbaa8c79fc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because the schematic depicts a main chain of glucose residues carrying a modest number of side chains, and this moderate degree of branching in a plant-starch (potato tuber) context matches amylopectin. Plant starch is a mixture of two glucose polymers, and amylopectin is the branched fraction (roughly 80% of the granule), built from an \(\alpha\text{-1,4}\)-linked backbone with periodic \(\alpha\text{-1,6}\) branch points. The reader must combine the topology drawn in the figure with the stated plant source to select it.

(Choice A) is incorrect: amylose is the other polymer packaged in plant starch granules, but it is the linear fraction, an \(\alpha\text{-1,4}\)-linked chain with no branch points, so a schematic showing side chains does not depict amylose.

(Choice C) is correct: amylopectin is the branched plant-starch polymer, and the main chain with a few side chains drawn in a potato-tuber (plant storage) context is consistent with its \(\alpha\text{-1,4}\) backbone plus scattered \(\alpha\text{-1,6}\) branches.

(Choice B) is incorrect: glycogen is the glucose storage polymer of animal muscle and liver, not plant tubers; it also carries far more frequent branch points than the polymer drawn, which shows only a few side chains.

(Choice D) is incorrect: cellulose is a structural polymer of the plant cell wall, not an energy-storage polymer of the granule; it is an unbranched \(\beta\text{-1,4}\)-linked chain forming extended linear fibers, inconsistent with the branched schematic.

As a Skill 2 item, this requires interpreting the branching topology in the figure and integrating it with the biological source to distinguish among four glucose polymers, rather than recalling a single definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'e3ed750c-47d8-48b1-8c6b-b9ef4339a4ed';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is D because saltatory conduction is faster than continuous conduction specifically because of where voltage-gated Na+ channels open, not how strongly or how large the response is. In an unmyelinated axon, "Continuous conduction is slow because there are always voltage-gated Na+ channels opening, and more and more Na+ is rushing into the cell" along the whole membrane, whereas in a myelinated axon "Saltatory conduction is faster because the action potential basically jumps from one node to the next" (A&P). Because voltage-gated channels are confined to the nodes of Ranvier, the depolarization spreads passively and rapidly across the insulated internodes and only regenerates at each node, so the impulse skips the slow, point-by-point channel opening required in continuous conduction. (Choice A) Myelin does not lower the threshold of excitation; the threshold is a fixed depolarization level, and speed comes from skipping internodal regeneration rather than from easier firing. (Choice B) Action potentials are all-or-none: "All action potentials peak at the same voltage (+30 mV), so one action potential is not bigger than another." The nodal spikes are therefore not larger in amplitude; the difference is where they fire, not how big they are. (Choice C) This reverses the text's logic: slowness in continuous conduction is attributed to constant channel opening with "more and more Na+ rushing into the cell," not to fast diffusion being a problem, and Na+ spread along the internode actually speeds saltatory conduction. (Choice D) Correct, because confining channel opening to widely spaced nodes lets the impulse leap between them rather than regenerating at every point, which is exactly why myelinated conduction outpaces continuous conduction. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires contrasting the channel behavior underlying two conduction modes and predicting which mechanistic difference explains the measured velocity difference between the two axons.$px$ WHERE id = 'e44c4572-3946-412c-937c-1c1d4a126687';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A researcher treats β-D-glucopyranose with a mild oxidizing agent that acts specifically on the ring position derived from the free aldehyde. Because the sugar was oxidized in its cyclic form, the initially generated carboxyl group remains held near the ring oxygen's parent hydroxyl, and an intramolecular reaction traps the product before it can equilibrate with the open-chain species. Which description best characterizes the isolated product?

(Recall that in β-D-glucopyranose the six-membered ring is closed by the hydroxyl on C5, and C6 remains a terminal -CH₂OH.)$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because oxidation targets the anomeric carbon (C1), the ring position derived from the open-chain aldehyde. In the open-chain form this would simply give the aldonic acid (D-gluconic acid), with C1 becoming a carboxyl \(\text{(-COOH)}\). But the sugar was oxidized while cyclic, so the newly formed carboxyl at C1 sits adjacent to the C5 hydroxyl, the same -OH that closes the pyranose ring. That C5 oxygen attacks the C1 carboxyl carbon intramolecularly, forming a cyclic ester (a lactone). Because C5 and C1 are separated so as to close a six-membered ring, the product is a δ-lactone (glucono-δ-lactone): a six-membered cyclic ester linking the C5 oxygen to the C1 carbonyl carbon.

(Choice D) is correct: a lactone is an intramolecular ester, and here the C5 -OH esterifies to the oxidized C1 carboxyl, giving the six-membered cyclic ester.

(Choice B) is incorrect: a C1-and-C6 dicarboxylic acid (glucaric/saccharic acid) requires oxidizing BOTH the anomeric carbon and the terminal \(\text{-CH}_2\text{OH}\) at C6. The mild reagent acts only at the aldehyde-derived C1, so C6 stays as an alcohol.

(Choice C) is incorrect: this describes oxidizing C6 to a carboxyl (glucuronic acid) while leaving the C1 hemiacetal intact. That is the reverse of what the reagent does. The reagent oxidizes C1, and C1 cannot remain a hemiacetal once it is oxidized.

(Choice A) is incorrect: converting C1 to a primary alcohol is a reduction (yielding the alditol glucitol/sorbitol), not an oxidation, and it produces an open-chain polyol with no ester or carboxyl group at all.

Under Skill 2, you must apply the mechanism of anomeric-carbon oxidation to a described cyclic substrate and predict that the geometrically proximal C5 hydroxyl closes onto the new carboxyl to yield a lactone, rather than defaulting to the familiar open-chain aldonic-acid answer.$px$ WHERE id = 'e45c97f6-a36b-45c4-83df-2693fb8e3a88';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$The Fischer projections shown display two aldohexoses, labeled A and B, each drawn with its aldehyde carbon (C1) at the top and its -CH2OH carbon (C6) at the bottom. Working down each chain from C1 to C6, compare the two structures. Which term correctly describes the stereochemical relationship between A and B, and at which numbered carbon does that relationship arise?

[FIGURE: Two aldohexose Fischer projections side by side, labeled A and B. Both have CHO at the top (C1) and CH2OH at the bottom (C6). Structure A, reading top to bottom, has -OH on the RIGHT at C2, on the LEFT at C3, on the RIGHT at C4, and on the RIGHT at C5. Structure B has -OH on the LEFT at C2, on the LEFT at C3, on the RIGHT at C4, and on the RIGHT at C5. The remaining position on each carbon is occupied by -H.]$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is A because tracing the two Fischer projections from C1 down to C6 shows that A and B place -OH on the same side at C3 (left, left), C4 (right, right), and C5 (right, right), and differ at only one stereocenter: at C2, structure A has -OH on the right while structure B has -OH on the left. Two sugars whose configurations match at every stereocenter but one are epimers, named for the carbon at which they differ; because the single transposition is at C2, A and B are C2 epimers. (Tracing the substituents shows A is D-glucose and B is D-mannose, the classic C2-epimer pair.)

(Choice A) is correct: Comparing the structures carbon by carbon, C3, C4, and C5 are identical and only the C2 substituents are swapped. A difference at exactly one stereocenter defines an epimeric relationship, and locating that center at C2 completes the answer.

(Choice B) is incorrect: C4 is where the D-glucose/D-galactose epimeric pair differs, and this is a common trap because galactose is the other well-known glucose epimer. In the projections shown, however, C4 carries -OH on the right in both structures; the transposed -H and -OH sit at C2, not C4.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Enantiomers are nonsuperimposable mirror images, which requires opposite configuration at every stereocenter (as in D-glucose versus L-glucose). Here the two structures match at C3, C4, and C5, so they cannot be mirror images.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Although epimers are a subset of diastereomers, this option specifies a difference at two stereocenters. Tracing the projections reveals a swap at only one carbon (C2), so calling the relationship a two-center difference misreads the structures.

At the Skill 2 level, you cannot answer by recalling a memorized fact; you must extract the configuration at each stereocenter from the Fischer projections, compare the two structures carbon by carbon, and map the observed single-center difference onto the correct isomer term and carbon number.$px$ WHERE id = 'e52179b5-4d2b-47c1-9316-4f3ff0c1444c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is B because diversifying selection favors two or more distinct, extreme phenotypes while selecting against the intermediate (average) phenotype, which increases the population's genetic variability. This is exactly the beach-mouse scenario: light-colored mice that blend in with the sand are favored, and dark-colored mice that can hide in the grass are also favored, while medium-colored mice match neither background and are most likely to be eaten by predators. The result is increased genetic variability as the population becomes more diverse, because two or more distinct phenotypes are selected for simultaneously. Because both extremes (not one end, and not the average) are favored here, the pattern is diversifying selection. (Choice A) is wrong because stabilizing selection favors an average phenotype and selects against extreme variation, producing a decrease in variability, the opposite of what is described. (Choice B) is correct: two extreme phenotypes are selected for while the intermediate is selected against, increasing variability. (Choice C) is wrong because directional selection selects for phenotypes at one end of the spectrum, shifting the population toward a single new phenotype, whereas here both ends are favored. (Choice D) is wrong because frequency-dependent selection favors phenotypes that are either common or rare, basing fitness on how common a phenotype is rather than on two fixed extreme phenotypes matching two backgrounds. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of diversifying selection and distinguish it from the other modes of natural selection.$px$ WHERE id = 'e52c6e27-0f79-4aa8-bb65-965e61de2a6b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because the pI is determined by averaging the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank the net-neutral (zwitterionic) species: the pair of ionizations closest to and on either side of the pI. For the acidic amino acid, the neutral form is bracketed by the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) and the acidic side chain, so \(\text{pI} = (2 + 4)/2 = 3.0\) (LOW). For the basic amino acid, the neutral form is bracketed by the \(\alpha\text{-NH}_3^+\) and the basic side chain, so \(\text{pI} = (9 + 12)/2 = 10.5\) (HIGH). The extra acidic group adds a low \(\text{pK}_a\) on the acid side of neutrality and the extra basic group adds a high \(\text{pK}_a\) on the base side, which is exactly why an acidic residue has a low \(\text{pI}\) and a basic residue a high \(\text{pI}\). (Choice A) is wrong: averaging all three [\((2+4+9)/3 = 5.0\) for the acid] dilutes in the unrelated third \(\text{pK}_a\) and does not give the pH where charge is zero. Only the two groups flanking neutrality count. (Choice B) is correct: averaging the two \(\text{pK}_a\) flanking the neutral form gives \(3.0\) for the acidic and \(10.5\) for the basic amino acid, reproducing the low-vs-high \(\text{pI}\) split. (Choice C) is wrong: for the acidic amino acid only one side chain is acidic, and forcing "the two side-chain values" mis-identifies which groups bracket neutrality; the neutral form here is flanked by the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) and the side chain, not by two side chains. (Choice D) is wrong: \(\text{pI}\) is a pH of net-zero charge obtained from a two-\(\text{pK}_a\) average, not from a single \(\text{pK}_a\); using the lowest alone [\(\approx 2\)] would assign nearly the same \(\text{pI}\) to both amino acids and erase the acidic-vs-basic difference. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the flanking-\(\text{pK}_a\) averaging rule to a given \(\text{pK}_a\) set to explain why an acidic residue's \(\text{pI}\) lies low and a basic residue's lies high.$px$ WHERE id = 'e56e7d5f-aa59-48e6-99e5-5564e0bf36a1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth.' Parturition is driven by a positive feedback loop: cervical stretch sends nerve impulses to the hypothalamus, which triggers oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary; oxytocin makes the uterine smooth muscle contract, which pushes the fetus against the cervix and stretches it still further. Because each contraction intensifies the very signal that caused it, contractions escalate rather than settling at a set point, and the loop is only broken when delivery removes the cervical stretch. (Choice A) is correct: contraction causes further cervical stretch, which drives still more oxytocin, so the output reinforces its own trigger. That is the defining feature of a positive feedback loop, and it is why the pattern escalates and stops only when the stretch (the stimulus) is removed at birth. (Choice B) is incorrect: opposing a rising signal to hold an output near a set point describes negative feedback, the predominant homeostatic mechanism (as in insulin lowering blood glucose); it would damp the contractions rather than let them intensify, which contradicts the escalating pattern observed. (Choice C) is incorrect: an open loop with no sensory return would give a fixed contraction strength set by the initial dose, but the recorded data show contractions growing stronger over time, which requires an amplifying feedback signal, not a fixed one. (Choice D) is incorrect: prostaglandins augment the contractions but do not replace oxytocin, and oxytocin actually rises rather than falls during labor; declining oxytocin cannot explain contractions that grow progressively more forceful. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of feedback loop structure to novel clinical data (escalating, stretch-dependent contractions) and infer that only self-amplifying positive feedback fits the observed pattern.$px$ WHERE id = 'e57dcb02-c0fa-4d06-9365-a67dd3ee9e3a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' Bond length is governed by two factors: for a bond between the same two atoms, adding shared electron pairs (higher bond order) pulls the nuclei closer and shortens the bond, so a triple bond is shorter than a single bond; and for bonds of the same order, larger atoms keep the nuclei farther apart, so a bond to an atom low in a group (like iodine, a large period-5 atom) is longer than a bond to a small period-2 atom. Ranking these three requires applying both ideas at once rather than counting shared pairs alone. (Choice A) is correct: the \(\text{C-I}\) single bond is longest because iodine is a large atom that holds the nuclei far apart despite being a single bond; the \(\text{C-C}\) single bond is intermediate (two small carbons, one shared pair); and the \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C}\) triple bond is shortest because three shared pairs draw the two small carbons closest together. (Choice B) is incorrect: it assumes bond order alone sets length, ranking the \(\text{C-C}\) single bond as longest and overlooking that iodine's large atomic size makes \(\text{C-I}\) longer than \(\text{C-C}\) even though both are single bonds. (Choice C) is incorrect: it inverts the bond-order/length relationship by placing the triple bond as longest; adding shared pairs shortens, not lengthens, a bond, so \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C}\) is the shortest of the three, not the longest. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly makes \(\text{C-C}\) longer than \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C}\) but mistakenly ranks the large-atom \(\text{C-I}\) bond as shortest, ignoring that iodine's size makes this single bond the longest of the set. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply two competing determinants of bond length (bond order and atomic size) to rank bonds that differ in both, rather than recall a single ordering.$px$ WHERE id = 'e59abddc-09e7-4ed5-8064-bcd2d936d046';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth.' During the reproductive sequence, the endometrium must be kept intact by progesterone for the pregnancy to continue, and the source of that progesterone shifts as gestation progresses. Early on, the chorion releases chorionic gonadotropin that keeps the corpus luteum active so it produces progesterone; later, the placenta takes over that endocrine role. (Choice A) is correct: during the second trimester the placenta assumes production of estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum, which has degenerated, so placental progesterone maintains the endometrium once the corpus luteum is gone. (Choice B) is incorrect: the corpus luteum does not persist throughout pregnancy. It has degenerated by the second trimester, which is precisely why another progesterone source is required. (Choice C) is incorrect: the endometrium is the target tissue that progesterone maintains, not an endocrine gland that manufactures its own progesterone; its upkeep depends on hormone supplied by the corpus luteum and then the placenta. (Choice D) is incorrect: chorionic gonadotropin does not substitute for progesterone at the endometrium. It acts on the corpus luteum to keep it active and thereby ensures adequate levels of progesterone to maintain the endometrium, so the endometrium still depends on progesterone. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the shift in the progesterone source during gestation to explain why the pregnancy survives the loss of the corpus luteum, rather than recalling a single stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = 'e7ad984f-1e62-41ad-b32e-51732aabb014';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is A because the cell described takes in matter (glucose, ions), releases matter (CO₂, waste), and exchanges heat with the fluid around it. Both matter and energy cross its boundary, which is the defining feature of an open system and the reason every living cell or organism is thermodynamically open.

(Choice B) is incorrect: a closed system exchanges energy but not matter; this cell plainly imports glucose and exports waste, so 'closed' ignores the matter it moves across its boundary.
(Choice C) is incorrect: an isolated system exchanges neither matter nor energy, but this cell exchanges both, making it the opposite of isolated.
(Choice A) is correct: because both matter and energy cross the boundary, the cell is an open system, the category that applies to all living things.
(Choice D) is incorrect: the cell does not merely recycle matter internally; it genuinely trades matter with the medium, so it cannot be classified as closed.

This item rewards Skill 2, Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving: classifying a system by identifying what actually crosses its boundary rather than recalling a bare definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'e7dd1c34-b38e-4b0a-8667-8c5a8f0ba723';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the equivalence point of any redox titration is defined by electron balance: the moles of electrons released by the reducing agent must equal the moles of electrons consumed by the oxidizing agent. Tracking oxidation numbers, Fe goes from \(+2\) to \(+3\), so each \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) donates exactly \(1\) electron; Mn goes from \(+7\) in \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) to \(+2\) in \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\), so each \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) accepts exactly \(5\) electrons. Setting electrons donated equal to electrons accepted, \(1 \times (\text{mol Fe}^{2+}) = 5 \times (\text{mol MnO}_4^-)\), which rearranges to \(5\) mol \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) reacting per \(1\) mol \(\text{MnO}_4^-\). Equivalence is therefore the point of equal electron transfer, not a one-to-one matching of titrant and analyte moles. (Choice A) This is the central misconception the question targets: titrant and analyte do not always react 1:1. A 1:1 mole match holds only when each species transfers the same number of electrons; here \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) transfers \(1\) and \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) transfers \(5\), so the equivalence point occurs at a 5:1 mole ratio (\(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) to \(\text{MnO}_4^-\)), not 1:1. (Choice C) The persistent faint purple is the endpoint, the experimentally observed signal that \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) is in slight excess and acts as its own indicator. The endpoint approximates but does not define the equivalence point; the equivalence point is the stoichiometric condition of equal electron transfer, an inherent property of the reaction rather than of the color change. (Choice D) This reverses the electron bookkeeping: it is \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) that accepts \(5\) electrons, while each \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) supplies only \(1\). Requiring five times as much \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) as \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) inverts the ratio; correct electron balance requires five times as much \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) as \(\text{MnO}_4^-\). Skill 3$px$ WHERE id = 'e86f6022-a426-402f-87b2-f9ef8bf361e0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is A because the described drug mimics cortisol, the principal glucocorticoid of the adrenal cortex (zona fasciculata), and glucocorticoids drive the catabolism of stored nutrients and stimulate gluconeogenesis to synthesize new glucose; chronically elevated glucocorticoid activity therefore raises circulating glucose, which is why patients on long-term steroids and those with cortisol excess (Cushing's) develop hyperglycemia. (Choice A) is correct: sustained glucocorticoid exposure promotes gluconeogenesis and inhibits tissue building, elevating blood glucose. (Choice B) is incorrect: lowering blood glucose is the action of insulin from the pancreas, not of a glucocorticoid, which raises glucose in the opposite direction. (Choice C) is incorrect: sodium regulation is governed by the mineralocorticoid aldosterone, which increases blood Na+ levels; it is a distinct adrenal cortical hormone and does not describe the glucose-raising, anti-inflammatory drug in question. (Choice D) is incorrect: glucocorticoids downregulate the immune system and inhibit the inflammatory response (the very reason the drug is prescribed), so they compromise, rather than heighten, antibody-based defenses, and chronically elevated levels impair resistance to infection. Applying knowledge of glucocorticoid function to a novel clinical scenario, one predicts the combined effect of elevated glucose alongside suppressed immunity, identifying elevated fasting blood glucose as the expected finding.$px$ WHERE id = 'e87597fb-1cd6-4b22-b5a6-9ae5413550f6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' The innermost germ layer identified in the stem is the endoderm, which forms the lining of the digestive tract (including the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas) and also the lining of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs of the respiratory tract. Answering requires knowing which other structures share that endodermal origin, rather than which structures are anatomically near or grossly similar to the intestine. (Choice A) is incorrect: the epidermis of the skin is the outer epithelial covering of the body surface and arises from the ectoderm, the outermost germ layer, not the endoderm. Being an epithelium is not enough to make a tissue endodermal. (Choice B) is incorrect: the muscular wall of the intestine is a trap because it belongs to the same organ as the gut lining, but specialized muscle tissue (including the muscles of the intestines) is a mesoderm derivative, not endoderm. (Choice C) is correct: the endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract and to the lining of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs, so the respiratory epithelium shares the same germ-layer origin as the small-intestinal lining. (Choice D) is incorrect: melanocytes and peripheral sensory neurons derive from the neural crest, an ectoderm-derived migratory population, not from the endoderm. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the specific adult structures that each primary germ layer produces and match a second structure to the endodermal lineage.$px$ WHERE id = 'e8a67e17-2993-47f3-8c99-43222d4dab6f';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is A because the aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) are the three residues whose side chains contain an aromatic functional group. These three residues make up the entire standard aromatic class: phenylalanine carries a benzene ring, tyrosine a phenol (a hydroxyl directly on the ring), and tryptophan a bicyclic indole. (Choice A) is correct: Phe, Tyr, and Trp are exactly the three side chains classified as aromatic. (Choice B) is incorrect: although histidine's imidazole and proline's 5-membered ring are ring structures, proline is classified as a nonpolar aliphatic amino acid with a non-aromatic ring, and histidine is grouped with the basic amino acids, not the aromatic class; only tryptophan in this set is aromatic. (Choice C) is incorrect: leucine, isoleucine, and valine are nonpolar aliphatic residues with straight or branched hydrocarbon chains, not aromatic rings. (Choice D) is incorrect: serine, threonine, and cysteine are polar uncharged residues whose side chains contain hydroxyl \(-\text{OH}\) or sulfhydryl \(-\text{SH}\) heteroatoms, not aromatic rings. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which amino acid side chains contain an aromatic ring.$px$ WHERE id = 'e8bae86b-a268-4835-b49f-56be1b0eedbb';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is B because hCG from the trophoblast is the signal that rescues the corpus luteum, and removing that signal lets the corpus luteum regress so progesterone falls and the endometrium is shed. After implantation the trophoblast secretes hCG, "a hormone that directs the corpus luteum to survive, enlarge, and continue producing progesterone and estrogen to suppress menses." If that signal is blocked, the situation mirrors a non-pregnant luteal phase: the corpus luteum "will stop secreting progesterone and degrade into the corpus albicans," and "without progesterone, the endometrium thins... endometrial tissue dies and blood, pieces of the endometrial tissue, and white blood cells are shed." With the endometrium shed, the implanted embryo cannot be maintained and the pregnancy fails. (Choice A) is wrong because during these early weeks "the pregnancy hormones are primarily generated by the corpus luteum," and the placenta only "gradually takes over... during weeks 12 to 17," so it cannot immediately compensate. (Choice B) is correct because blocking hCG removes the rescue signal, causing luteal regression, falling progesterone, endometrial breakdown, and loss of the pregnancy. (Choice C) is wrong because corpus luteum maintenance after implantation depends on hCG, not on pituitary LH, and the high progesterone of the luteal phase "triggers negative feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary, which keeps GnRH, LH, and FSH secretions low," so no rescuing LH surge occurs. (Choice D) is wrong because the regressing corpus luteum stops producing both progesterone and estrogen, so estrogen falls rather than rising to thicken the endometrium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting a multi-step downstream physiological consequence of a pharmacological perturbation rather than recalling a stated fact.$px$ WHERE id = 'e8cecba1-6411-45e1-8860-4272779e32dc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$For the photoelectric effect, \(K_\text{max} = h(f - f_0)\), so a plot of \(K_\text{max}\) versus \(f\) is a straight line whose slope is Planck's constant and whose frequency-axis intercept (where \(K_\text{max} = 0\)) is the threshold \(f_0\). Using the two highest data points, the slope is \(\frac{(3.48 - 0.83)\times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}}{(1.1\times 10^{15} - 7.0\times 10^{14})\ \text{Hz}} = \frac{2.65\times 10^{-19}}{4.0\times 10^{14}} \approx 6.6 \times 10^{-34}\ \text{J·s}\), which confirms the slope is Planck's constant. Extrapolating down from \((7.0\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz},\ 0.83\times 10^{-19}\ \text{J})\), \(K_\text{max}\) reaches zero after another \(\frac{0.83\times 10^{-19}}{6.6\times 10^{-34}} \approx 1.26\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\), giving \(f_0 \approx 7.0\times 10^{14} - 1.26\times 10^{14} \approx 5.75\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\). The listed value nearest this intercept is \(5.5\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\). (Choice B) is correct: \(5.5\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) is the option closest to the extrapolated intercept \(f_0 \approx 5.75\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) where \(K_\text{max}\) falls to zero. (Choice A) \(5.0\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) is a frequency at which no electrons are ejected at all, so it lies below the threshold rather than at it; the extrapolated intercept where \(K_\text{max}\) reaches zero is higher, near \(5.75\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\), and \(5.5\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) sits closer to that value. (Choice C) \(6.3\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) does fall inside the correct bracket, between the no-current frequency (\(5.0\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\)) and the first current-producing frequency (\(7.0\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\)), but knowing only that \(f_0\) lies between those two values is not enough; extrapolating the fitted line to \(K_\text{max} = 0\) places the intercept well below the middle of that range, near \(5.75\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\), so \(6.3\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) overestimates it. (Choice D) \(7.0\times 10^{14}\ \text{Hz}\) already ejects electrons with \(K_\text{max} = 0.83\times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}\), so it lies above the threshold, not at it; a positive \(K_\text{max}\) means the photon energy there already exceeds the work function. Skill 4 is required because \(f_0\) is never one of the measured frequencies: the data must be treated as a linear trend and extended back to the \(K_\text{max} = 0\) intercept rather than read directly off any single data point.$px$ WHERE id = 'e93f3e35-eec1-43b3-969b-c2c1b1817826';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because vital capacity is the sum of inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume, and expiratory reserve volume (it excludes residual volume, which cannot be exhaled). Before: 3,000 + 500 + 1,100 = 4,600 mL. After: 1,800 + 500 + 700 = 3,000 mL. The change is 3,000 − 4,600 = −1,600 mL, a decrease of 1,600 mL, driven by the reduced inspiratory and expiratory reserves as the stiffer lungs resist expansion.

(Choice B) is correct: VC = IRV + TV + ERV = 4,600 mL initially and 3,000 mL afterward, so it falls by exactly 1,600 mL, matching the combined drop in the two reserve volumes (1,200 mL + 400 mL) while TV is unchanged.

(Choice A) is incorrect: 1,200 mL is only the fall in inspiratory reserve volume (3,000 → 1,800). It ignores the 400 mL drop in expiratory reserve volume, which also counts toward vital capacity.

(Choice C) is incorrect: 2,800 mL comes from subtracting the new vital capacity (3,000 mL) from the original total lung capacity (5,800 mL), or otherwise folding residual volume into the difference. Residual volume (1,200 mL) is unchanged here and is never part of vital capacity, so it must not appear in the change.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the direction is reversed. Stiffer, less-expandable lungs cannot draw in or push out as much air, so the reserve volumes and therefore vital capacity must fall, not rise.

This item tests the skill of reasoning from multi-condition volume data to compute how a defined capacity changes.$px$ WHERE id = 'e9d3d1bb-a18c-4c07-ab7f-d256e4bbe6a8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses.' Serotonin is a biogenic amine whose only route of removal from the synaptic cleft is reuptake: it is transported back into the presynaptic cell for repackaging, with reuptake by neurons serving as the elimination mechanism for biogenic amines and no cleft-degrading enzyme involved. Because a neurotransmitter's effect persists only while it remains bound to its receptors, blocking the sole clearance transporter lets serotonin accumulate and keep re-binding. (Choice A) is correct: with reuptake blocked and no enzyme to substitute, serotonin lingers in the cleft and continues binding its receptors, so the postsynaptic response lasts longer and is stronger than at the untreated synapse. (Choice B) is incorrect: although serotonergic terminals do have autoreceptors, the immediate pharmacological effect of blocking clearance is a net rise in cleft serotonin; presynaptic feedback modulates but does not fully cancel the accumulation, so signaling does not simply return to baseline. (Choice C) is incorrect: it confuses clearance with release. Reuptake recycles transmitter for later use, but vesicular release is a separate \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-triggered exocytosis step, and the drug acts fast enough that impaired recycling does not starve the terminal or reduce signaling. (Choice D) is incorrect: accumulating serotonin binds the same receptors more, prolonging and strengthening the existing response; the sign of the effect is set by the receptor type, so simply having more transmitter does not flip an excitatory synapse into an inhibitory one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between a transmitter's clearance route and its receptor-binding duration to predict the downstream effect of blocking that route.$px$ WHERE id = 'ea28b186-88de-4b7c-acd2-b0151f88ca0c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because microfilament-based force generation requires actin filaments to slide against the myosin motor, so inactivating myosin abolishes active cortical contraction, whereas the microvillus is held up by the actin bundle itself and so remains supported even without a working motor. Actin polymerizes into filaments that serve as tracks along which the motor protein myosin moves, and contraction occurs when actin and myosin filaments slide past one another. Contraction is the product of the actin-myosin interaction, not of actin alone. Microfilaments also provide rigidity and shape to the cell, a structural role the polymerized actin bundle fills without needing the motor; in the intestine these actin-cored projections are the microvilli that increase the surface area available for absorption. Therefore removing only the motor splits the two functions: the motor-driven cortical contraction fails, but the static actin scaffold of the microvillus persists. (Choice A) is wrong because it treats both functions as motor-dependent; the actin core still provides rigidity and shape without myosin, so the microvilli do not collapse. (Choice B) reverses the dependency. It is the contraction, not the static microvillus bundle, that needs myosin, so preserving contraction while losing microvilli is backwards. (Choice C) is the tempting partial truth: actin is intact, but contraction arises only when actin and myosin filaments slide past each other, so actin cannot generate contractile force on its own without the motor. (Choice D) correctly separates motor-dependent force generation from the motor-independent structural scaffold. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that microfilament force generation depends on the actin-myosin interaction to predict which of two distinct actin-based functions survives selective loss of the motor.$px$ WHERE id = 'ea2ec9c7-cb92-4d95-b06e-63eeb14d60c8';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is C because a son receives his single X chromosome from his mother and his Y chromosome from his father, so his phenotype depends only on which of the mother's two X chromosomes he inherits. The carrier mother \(X^H X^h\) passes \(X^H\) to half of her sons and \(X^h\) to the other half; a son who receives \(X^h\) is hemizygous and therefore affected, while a son who receives \(X^H\) is unaffected. Working the cross \(X^H X^h \times X^H Y\), the four equally likely offspring boxes are \(X^H X^H\), \(X^H X^h\), \(X^H Y\), and \(X^h Y\); among the two sons (\(X^H Y\) and \(X^h Y\)), exactly one is affected, giving a \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) probability. A carrier female passes the disease allele to half of her sons, so each male offspring of a carrier of a recessive X-linked disorder has a 50 percent chance of being affected. (Choice A) \(\tfrac{1}{4}\) counts the one affected son against all four offspring boxes; this is the unconditional chance that a child is an affected son, but the question already conditions on the child being a son, so the denominator should be the two sons, not all four offspring. (Choice B) \(0\) reverses the source of the son's X chromosome. It assumes the son's sex chromosome that carries the disease comes from the father, but the father contributes only the Y, and the son's single X (and thus his disease status) comes entirely from the carrier mother. (Choice C) is correct: half of the carrier mother's sons inherit her \(X^h\) allele and, being hemizygous, are affected, so the probability is \(\tfrac{1}{2}\). (Choice D) \(\tfrac{3}{4}\) misapplies the autosomal \(3{:}1\) dominant-to-recessive phenotype ratio from a monohybrid cross; that ratio does not apply because males are hemizygous, having only one allele for an X-linked gene. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rules of X-linked transmission to a specific carrier-by-unaffected cross and compute the probability for the male offspring.$px$ WHERE id = 'ea3d12de-d917-47d7-9bf6-74bb6a177cdb';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is A because thermodynamics and kinetics are independent. A large negative \(\Delta G\) tells you the reaction is exergonic (energetically favorable and capable of releasing free energy), and it fixes the position of equilibrium, but it says nothing about how quickly the reaction reaches that equilibrium. The rate is governed by the height of the activation-energy barrier (\(E_a\)) that separates reactants from the transition state. Glucose is thermodynamically unstable toward oxidation yet kinetically stable: its activation barrier is so high that at 37 °C essentially no molecules carry enough energy to cross it, so the oxidation crawls until a catalyst (in cells, an enzyme) lowers \(E_a\) and speeds the reaction up without changing \(\Delta G\).

(Choice A) is correct: \(\Delta G\) sets favorability and the energy available, but not the rate; the slow oxidation reflects a high activation barrier that a catalyst can lower without altering \(\Delta G\).

(Choice B) is incorrect: 'spontaneous' is a thermodynamic label meaning \(\Delta G < 0\), not a claim about speed. A reaction can be spontaneous yet immeasurably slow, so the lack of detectable oxidation does not make \(\Delta G\) positive or the process non-spontaneous.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the magnitude of \(\Delta G\) does not determine the rate. \(\Delta G\) and \(E_a\) are independent quantities, so a strongly negative \(\Delta G\) does not force a low activation barrier or guarantee a fast reaction.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it confuses the activation energy with \(\Delta G\). The barrier the molecules must surmount is \(E_a\), measured from the reactants up to the transition state, not \(\Delta G\), which is only the reactant-to-product energy difference; a large \(|\Delta G|\) does not by itself imply a large \(E_a\).

This item is Skill 2: it requires distinguishing thermodynamic spontaneity (governed by \(\Delta G\)) from reaction rate (governed by activation energy) and reasoning about why an energetically favorable reaction can still be slow.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\Delta G\\) tells you only whether the reaction is favorable and how much energy it can release, not how fast it goes. The rate is instead set by the activation-energy barrier, which here is high enough to keep the oxidation immeasurably slow without a catalyst."},{"label":"B","text":"The absence of any detectable reaction shows the process is not truly spontaneous under these conditions; a genuinely negative \\(\\Delta G\\) would drive the mixture essentially to completion the instant the reactants were combined."},{"label":"C","text":"The rate of an exergonic reaction is fixed by the magnitude of its \\(\\Delta G\\): the more negative \\(\\Delta G\\) becomes, the lower its activation barrier must be, so a reaction as exergonic as \\(-2870\\) kJ/mol cannot possibly be slow, and the reported observation must reflect an experimental error."},{"label":"D","text":"The reaction is slow because its reactants must first climb an energy barrier equal in size to \\(\\Delta G\\); since \\(\\Delta G\\) here is very large in magnitude, that barrier is enormous, so a large \\(\\Delta G\\) always makes a reaction slow."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'ea3d7976-0ea1-4dfd-85d0-75ebc6e03eab';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because a gas is perfusion-limited when its capillary partial pressure rises to meet the alveolar value partway along the capillary: once equilibrium is reached, no further net transfer occurs, so the amount taken up over a given time is set by how much blood is delivered (perfusion). Oxygen behaves this way in a healthy lung, equilibrating after roughly a third of the transit. A gas is diffusion-limited when its capillary partial pressure never rises enough to shrink the alveolar-to-capillary gradient, so the gradient, and thus net transfer, persists for the whole transit and uptake is capped only by the membrane's diffusing capacity. Carbon monoxide behaves this way because hemoglobin binds it so avidly that essentially all CO entering the plasma is pulled onto hemoglobin, keeping the dissolved (free) capillary partial pressure near zero throughout.
(Choice A) is correct: it correctly pairs O2 with perfusion-limitation (early equilibration means flow determines uptake) and CO with diffusion-limitation (a persistently near-zero capillary partial pressure keeps the gradient maximal, so only diffusing capacity limits uptake).
(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses both classifications. O2 is not diffusion-limited in a healthy lung precisely because it equilibrates early, and CO is not perfusion-limited because raising blood flow does not relieve its limiting factor. Its uptake is set by diffusion, not delivery.
(Choice C) is incorrect: binding hemoglobin does not by itself make a gas perfusion-limited. What matters is whether the capillary partial pressure rises to meet the alveolar value; for CO it does not, so its uptake is limited by diffusion, and increasing cardiac output would not raise CO uptake the way it raises O2 uptake.
(Choice D) is incorrect: sharing the same membrane does not make both gases equally diffusion-limited. In a healthy lung O2 equilibrates well before the end of the capillary, making its uptake flow-dependent rather than membrane-thickness-dependent, so membrane thickness does not set O2 and CO uptake equally.
This question tests the skill of reasoning from a described physiological scenario to classify gas transfer as diffusion- versus perfusion-limited and identify the correct underlying mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = 'ea59948b-bb57-44ab-9f3d-18d05e1a8ac0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Mechanisms of Hormone Action.' The answer is D because the loop described is a positive feedback system: the hormone's own action (stronger contractions) intensifies the original stimulus (cervical stretch), which drives release of still more hormone, so secretion is self-reinforcing and accelerates rather than being opposed. Such a loop moves the system progressively farther from its starting state until a definite end point (delivery of the infant) removes the stimulus and halts release. (Choice D) is correct: in positive feedback the product amplifies the very change that triggered it, producing a runaway surge that continues to escalate until an external endpoint interrupts the cycle. (Choice B) is incorrect: suppression of further secretion once a hormone reaches an adequate blood level defines negative feedback, the opposite arrangement, which stabilizes rather than amplifies output. (Choice C) is incorrect: maintaining a variable near a fixed set point by correcting deviations is the behavior of a negative-feedback homeostatic loop, not of a self-amplifying cascade that has no stabilizing set point. (Choice A) is incorrect: a response that reverses its own triggering stimulus opposes the initial change and thereby restores the resting state, which is again negative feedback and contradicts the amplifying structure described. Recognizing this requires Skill 2 reasoning: the student must analyze the causal chain in the scenario, infer that action feeds back to amplify its own cause, and predict the resulting escalating behavior rather than recall a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'ea8c1483-3da9-4020-a000-b7df8a595ed7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' A Lewis base donates a lone pair, a Lewis acid accepts it, and the product is a Lewis acid-base adduct; a stronger Lewis base can displace a weaker one from the same acid. (Choice B) is correct: in the first adduct, the ether oxygen donated a lone pair to the electron-deficient boron, so ether is a Lewis base bound to the Lewis acid \(\text{BF}_3\); when the stronger base \(\text{NH}_3\) donates its nitrogen lone pair to that same boron, the ether's oxygen lone pair is pushed off, so ether is the Lewis base that gets displaced. (Choice A) is incorrect: ether does bind boron, but as the electron-pair donor it is the Lewis base, not the acid. The acid is \(\text{BF}_3\), whose boron accepts the pair. (Choice C) is incorrect: no proton moves in this step; boron, not hydrogen, is transferred between bases, so the Brønsted framework does not apply and ether is not accepting an \(\text{H}^+\). (Choice D) is incorrect: this reverses the chemistry. The incoming stronger base \(\text{NH}_3\) is what displaces the ether, and both ether and ammonia are electron-pair donors (Lewis bases), not acids. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lewis definitions to a displacement scenario and infer which reactant loses its bond to boron.$px$ WHERE id = 'eb7d1692-7242-447b-abdd-fc7ab260744c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Biosignaling.'

The answer is A because the switch-II glutamine positions the catalytic water that hydrolyzes the terminal (γ) phosphate of bound GTP; removing it produces a GTPase-dead subunit that cannot convert its bound GTP to GDP. Since the GTP-loaded conformation is the one that engages and stimulates the effector, the subunit stays "on" indefinitely. The data show exactly this: in WT, messenger falls back toward baseline within minutes of agonist washout (95 → 4) because each activated subunit hydrolyzes its GTP and switches off, whereas in Q→L the messenger stays high (98 → 88) long after the agonist is gone. That is signaling that fails to terminate rather than signaling that is stronger at the peak. The peaks (95 vs 98) are essentially identical; the divergence is entirely in how the signal decays, which is the signature of a broken off-switch, not a stronger on-switch.

(Choice A) is correct: it correctly ties the loss of γ-phosphate cleavage to a persistently GTP-bound, effector-stimulating subunit that keeps producing messenger even after the receptor stops loading new subunits, which matches the sustained plateau.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the active/inactive relationship. The GTP-bound (not GDP-bound) conformation stimulates the effector; a GDP-loaded subunit is the "off" state and would give a rapid return to baseline, the opposite of the observed plateau.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it misattributes the sustained output to the βγ dimer and invents a GDP→GTP exchange block. The mutation is in the catalytic residue for hydrolysis, not in the nucleotide-exchange step; and the persistence here reflects an α subunit trapped in its GTP-bound active state, not free βγ.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it inverts the kinetics. Faster hydrolysis would switch the subunit off sooner and speed the return to baseline, producing a decay faster than WT rather than the flat, elevated trace observed.

To reach the answer you had to reason from the pattern in the data (identical peaks but a WT curve that decays and a mutant curve that does not) and infer that the defect lies in the mechanism that turns the signal off (γ-phosphate hydrolysis), rather than accepting any single restated definition of the subunit's activity.$px$ WHERE id = 'ebabb63a-1661-46d5-98bc-27e82f006818';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because the described state (low insulin, high fatty-acid flux, and acetyl-CoA outpacing the oxaloacetate needed for TCA entry) is precisely the trigger for ketogenesis: the liver diverts excess acetyl-CoA into ketone bodies and exports them, and over days the brain up-regulates the machinery to oxidize ketones so it can spare glucose. The other outputs contradict the low-insulin, fuel-mobilizing conditions given.

(Choice C) is correct: low insulin plus surplus mitochondrial acetyl-CoA drives hepatic ketogenesis, and the brain adaptively shifts toward ketone oxidation during prolonged fasting.
(Choice A) is incorrect: glycogen synthesis is an insulin-driven fed-state anabolic process; with insulin low and stores already tapped, the liver is mobilizing, not storing, so this reverses the hormonal logic. Incoming acetyl-CoA also cannot make net glucose or glycogen in humans.
(Choice B) is incorrect: this predicts the opposite of the actual response. The state described is exactly what raises ketogenesis, and suppressing it would leave the glucose-limited brain without its adaptive alternative fuel.
(Choice D) is incorrect: fatty-acid synthesis is a fed-state, high-insulin, cytosolic malonyl-CoA-driven pathway; malonyl-CoA would block \(\beta\)-oxidation entirely, which is incompatible with the high fatty-acid oxidation flux the stem specifies.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must map a described hormonal and substrate state to the correct pathway output rather than recall a definition of ketogenesis.$px$ WHERE id = 'ec0c4e0a-c4d8-4594-b713-159d8b587c06';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' Prokaryotes exchange genes by three classic horizontal-transfer routes that differ in their physical requirements: transformation depends on naked DNA being present in the medium, conjugation requires direct cell-to-cell contact through a pilus, and transduction moves DNA between cells packaged inside a bacteriophage. Because each route has a distinguishing physical property, the three experimental treatments together fingerprint the mechanism rather than relying on any single result. (Choice A) is correct: DNase resistance shows the transferred DNA is shielded from nuclease (consistent with being inside a phage capsid, not naked); transfer across a filter that blocks cells but passes smaller particles shows no cell-to-cell contact is needed yet a small particle must cross; and loss of transfer after antiserum inactivates the phages pins the vector specifically to bacteriophages. This is transduction, in which a bacteriophage carrying a small fragment of DNA picked up from one prokaryote injects that DNA into a different cell. (Choice B) is incorrect: transformation requires the recipient to take up naked DNA released into the environment, but naked DNA would be degraded by DNase, so a DNase-resistant transfer rules transformation out. (Choice C) is incorrect: conjugation transfers genes through a hollow tube, or pilus, that requires the two cells to be drawn into direct contact, so transfer that still occurs when a filter physically separates the cells excludes conjugation. (Choice D) is incorrect: transposition relocates a mobile element within or between DNA molecules inside a single cell and does not itself move a gene from one separate cell to another, so it cannot explain donor-to-recipient transfer that depends on an external phage vector. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinguishing physical properties of each transfer route to a set of experimental results and infer which mechanism is operating.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"Transduction: the resistance gene is delivered from donor to recipient inside a viral particle"},{"label":"B","text":"Transformation: the recipient takes up free resistance-gene DNA from the surrounding medium"},{"label":"C","text":"Conjugation: the resistance gene passes through a pilus during direct donor-recipient contact"},{"label":"D","text":"Transposition: the resistance gene relocates within the recipient's own genome via a mobile element"}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'ec2521ef-c5fd-4314-abfd-be57c2a9ba53';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because the schematic depicts a single continuous chain of glucose beads with no points where a second chain emerges from a residue. A glucose polymer has exactly one reducing end (the terminus whose anomeric carbon is free) and one non-reducing end for each chain terminus that is not the reducing end. A chain with no branch points therefore terminates in only two ends total, one reducing and one non-reducing, so it presents a single non-reducing end to the exoglycosidase. Because degradative enzymes such as glycogen phosphorylase liberate glucose one residue at a time, working inward from each non-reducing end, the number of non-reducing ends sets how many places the enzyme can act simultaneously. A glycogen fragment of the same size carries many branch points, and each branch adds another non-reducing terminus; with many ends available at once, glucose is released rapidly. The polymer in the schematic, with only one such terminus, is degraded from one point only and mobilizes glucose more slowly.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it inverts the topology read from the schematic. "Many non-reducing ends" describes a branched molecule like glycogen, but the schematic shows no branch points, so this end count does not match the structure, and the faster rate that would follow from it does not apply.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly counts one non-reducing end but pairs it with the wrong rate. A single point of enzyme attack cannot release glucose faster than a molecule offering many simultaneous points; one end predicts slower, not faster, mobilization.

(Choice C) is correct: the schematic shows one continuous unbranched chain, which has a single non-reducing end, and a single point of exoglycosidic attack means glucose is freed more slowly than from an equally sized glycogen fragment whose many branches expose many non-reducing ends at once.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it lands on the correct slower rate but by faulty reasoning, assigning "many non-reducing ends" to a structure the schematic shows to be unbranched. Many ends would predict faster, not slower, release, so the end count and the rate are internally inconsistent.

At the Skill 2 level, answering requires reading the branching topology directly from the schematic (counting chain termini rather than recalling a definition) and then reasoning from that structural feature to a functional consequence: fewer non-reducing ends means fewer simultaneous sites of enzymatic glucose release, hence a slower mobilization rate.$px$ WHERE id = 'ec75aadd-d900-4c02-b02c-7d40aaa6a249';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is A because the described location and substrate pin down the enzyme. An enzyme sitting on the luminal surface of capillary endothelium and acting on triacylglycerol carried inside circulating particles is lipoprotein lipase (LPL). LPL frees fatty acids from the triacylglycerol inside those particles so the neighboring tissue can take them up. The key discriminator is where the enzyme acts and on which triacylglycerol pool: circulating (transported) versus stored.

(Choice A) is correct: lipoprotein lipase is positioned on the capillary endothelium and hydrolyzes TAG carried inside circulating particles, delivering fatty acids to the tissue, which is exactly the scenario described.
(Choice B) is incorrect: the role described (acting on circulating particles at the capillary surface) is correct, but the enzyme is misnamed. Hormone-sensitive lipase does not sit on the endothelium acting on circulating particles.
(Choice C) is incorrect: hormone-sensitive lipase does act on stored intracellular TAG, but the stem places the enzyme on the capillary surface acting on circulating particles, which is not what HSL does.
(Choice D) is incorrect: pancreatic lipase works in the intestinal lumen on dietary fat, not on the capillary endothelium acting on triacylglycerol already inside circulating particles.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, it requires mapping a described location and substrate pool to the correct enzyme rather than recalling enzyme names from a list.$px$ WHERE id = 'ecd55519-61d8-4094-9536-8ceda50290d1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Mechanisms of Development'. The answer is C because differentiation is abolished by the one thing the filter controls (physical membrane contact), even though the filter still allows molecules dissolved in the surrounding fluid to pass, which points to a signal carried only through channels that form where neighboring membranes connect. The four signaling categories are distinguished chiefly by how the signal travels: in direct signaling across gap junctions, fluid-filled channels allow small signaling molecules called intracellular mediators to diffuse between the two cells, and these channels are connections between the plasma membranes of adjacent cells, so the mediator never enters the open extracellular space and cannot cross a filter that blocks membrane contact. By contrast, paracrine, endocrine, and autocrine ligands are all released into the extracellular fluid, so a soluble-permeable filter would not stop them from reaching the other side. Because the response returns only when the membranes connect and the channels can form, the data isolate cytoplasmic continuity through touching membranes, not free diffusion through the fluid, as the necessary variable. (Choice A) A paracrine ligand moves by diffusion through the extracellular matrix, so it would dissolve in the surrounding fluid and freely cross the filter, and the response would not be abolished. (Choice B) Endocrine hormones travel the large distances between endocrine cells and their target cells via the bloodstream, a soluble long-distance mode that both contradicts the contact requirement and is irrelevant in a chamber with no circulation. (Choice C) Gap-junction signaling transfers an intracellular mediator only through channels between connected membranes, exactly matching a response that fails through the filter but recovers on direct contact. (Choice D) An autocrine signal binds receptors on the same cell that released it, so it does not transfer to a separate responder population and cannot explain induction of the neighboring responder cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting the outcome of a controlled chamber experiment to infer which physical route the inducing signal must take.$px$ WHERE id = 'ed29cf29-f7eb-4d29-962b-22862a07e3c8';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A student claims that ATP powers cellular work because its terminal phosphoanhydride bond "physically stores" a large packet of energy that is released only when that specific bond breaks, as if the bond itself were a compressed spring. Their instructor responds that this is a misconception: bond breaking always REQUIRES energy, and the large negative \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) of ATP hydrolysis actually comes from properties of the products relative to the reactants. Which observation BEST supports the instructor's corrected explanation for why ATP hydrolysis is strongly exergonic?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Principles of Bioenergetics.'

The answer is D because the large negative \(\Delta G^{\circ\prime}\) of ATP hydrolysis is a PRODUCT-side effect, not energy hidden in a special bond. At physiological pH, ATP carries several closely spaced negative charges that repel one another; splitting off \(P_i\) relieves that charge repulsion. In addition, the freed inorganic phosphate is stabilized by resonance across its several equivalent oxygens and is more extensively solvated (hydrated) than it was as part of ATP. These three product-favoring factors (charge-repulsion relief, resonance stabilization of \(P_i\), and greater solvation of the products) make the products lower in free energy than the reactants, which is what makes hydrolysis exergonic.

(Choice D) is correct: it names the actual product-stabilizing factors (relief of electrostatic repulsion, resonance stabilization of \(P_i\), and enhanced solvation) that lower the free energy of ADP + \(P_i\) relative to ATP.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it restates the very misconception the instructor is correcting: that a bond is a container of energy released "intact." Bond breaking always costs energy; the favorability comes from what the products look like afterward, not from a spring-loaded bond.

(Choice B) is incorrect: water is not an unusually high-energy reactant, and hydrolysis is not automatically exergonic for every phosphate compound (for example, hydrolyzing glucose-6-phosphate is far less exergonic, about -13.8 kJ/mol versus -30.5 kJ/mol for ATP). The magnitude depends on how much more stable the specific products are.

(Choice C) is incorrect: cleaving a bond is endergonic (it requires input of energy); no bond-breaking step is itself energy-releasing. The overall reaction is exergonic only because new bonds form and the products are strongly stabilized.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: applying the thermodynamic principle that \(\Delta G\) is set by the free-energy difference between products and reactants, so the source of ATP's "high-energy" character must be located in product stabilization rather than in a mythical energy-rich bond.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"The terminal phosphoanhydride bond holds an unusually large packet of stored energy that is liberated fully intact at the very instant the bond is cleaved, independent of the products."},{"label":"B","text":"Water is such a high-energy reactant that adding it to essentially any phosphate ester automatically makes the resulting hydrolysis strongly exergonic, no matter how stable the products are."},{"label":"C","text":"Cleaving the terminal phosphorus-oxygen bond is itself an exergonic, energy-releasing step, and that release upon bond breaking is precisely what makes the overall hydrolysis favorable."},{"label":"D","text":"The separated ADP and \\(P_i\\) products experience less mutual electrostatic repulsion than intact ATP did, and the freed \\(P_i\\) gains additional resonance stabilization and more extensive solvation."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'eebb7d42-8852-4b56-93d4-d713c56a694e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is A because the two products of adipose lipolysis are handled differently by the body. Glycerol is a three-carbon backbone that the liver feeds into gluconeogenesis to help maintain blood glucose during the fast, whereas the free fatty acids are taken up by tissues such as skeletal muscle and the liver and oxidized through \(\beta\)-oxidation to generate ATP. This division of labor across organs is the integrated fasting response.

(Choice A) is correct: glycerol serves as a gluconeogenic substrate in the liver, while the fatty acids are oxidized for ATP by muscle and other tissues.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the two fates. Even-chain fatty acids cannot be converted into net glucose, and it is glycerol, not the fatty acids, that supplies carbon for hepatic gluconeogenesis.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the brain cannot oxidize free fatty acids because they do not cross the blood-brain barrier, and it does not burn glycerol directly; the brain relies on glucose, using ketones only in prolonged starvation.

(Choice D) is incorrect: re-esterifying the released products back into stored triacylglycerol is a fed, high-insulin storage response; during a fast the tissues consume these products rather than re-store them.

This item tests Skill 2 (scientific reasoning and problem-solving): you reason from the chemical properties of glycerol and fatty acids to their distinct fates across liver and muscle during a fast.$px$ WHERE id = 'efd20aec-eadb-45ee-aeb0-1bab4ba8c2c0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the A band equals the fixed length of the thick filaments and never changes, while the thin filaments sliding fully inward erase the I band and the H zone. As the sarcomere shortens, the Z lines move closer together and the I band gets smaller, the A band stays the same width, and at full contraction the thin and thick filaments overlap completely; the H zone (the central region of the A band that contains only thick filaments) is shortened during contraction as the inward-moving thin filaments fill it, and the I band (which contains only thin filaments) also shortens. Because the thick filaments themselves do not shorten, the A band that they define holds its \(1.6\ \mu m\) width even as the overlapping regions are squeezed out. (Choice A) Correct: the A band is set by thick-filament length and is preserved, while complete inward sliding of thin filaments eliminates both the thin-only I band and the thick-only H zone, the two regions that vanish at maximal overlap. (Choice B) Reversed: the A band does not shorten and remains the same width, so it is the I band and H zone, not the A band, that disappear. (Choice C) Misconception: the filaments do not shorten but slide past one another, so the bands do not scale down proportionally; only the I band and H zone shrink while the A band is unchanged. (Choice D) Partial truth: the I band does disappear, but the H zone is shortened during contraction rather than widening, because the inward-sliding thin filaments invade the central thick-only region. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated resting band widths and reason from them to the predicted contracted banding pattern.$px$ WHERE id = 'efe40776-5758-484d-981e-009789333481';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because oxygen loading depends not only on an adequate partial-pressure gradient but also on the red blood cell spending enough time in the capillary for its PO2 to equilibrate with alveolar air; when destruction of the capillary bed forces the whole cardiac output through fewer vessels, each cell traverses the exchange region far faster, and if transit time falls below the time required to reach equilibrium, the cell exits still incompletely oxygenated despite a normal gradient and normal membrane. (Choice D) is correct: it correctly identifies that inadequate contact (transit) time, not an inadequate gradient or barrier, prevents capillary PO2 from reaching alveolar PO2. (Choice A) is incorrect: the alveolar PO2 is stated to be unchanged and capillary blood arrives at its usual low PO2, so the driving gradient is intact. The problem is the time available to act on that gradient, not the gradient itself. (Choice B) is incorrect: pulmonary capillary flow remains laminar at these velocities, and turbulence does not form a barrier to diffusion; oxygen crosses the membrane by dissolving and diffusing down its partial-pressure gradient regardless of flow character. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem specifies that membrane thickness is unchanged, and losing capillaries does not deposit tissue that lengthens the diffusion path. This describes a distance-limited defect rather than the transit-time defect actually at work. This item tests the skill of reasoning about how perfusion dynamics and equilibration kinetics, not just the diffusion gradient, determine the completeness of alveolar gas exchange.$px$ WHERE id = 'f08dcdff-f33e-4f62-bc65-efe57e2060f9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because the six-membered pyranose ring adopts a chair rather than a flat ring, and each ring carbon bears one substituent that points roughly outward from the ring (equatorial) and one that points straight up or down (axial). In the structure shown, the bulky groups (the \(-\text{OH}\) groups and the \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) group) are the ones angled outward, so they occupy equatorial positions, while the small hydrogens take the axial up/down positions. An equatorial substituent points away from the other ring atoms, so it experiences far less steric strain (1,3-diaxial crowding) than it would in an axial position, where it would be forced close to the axial substituents two carbons away. β-D-glucopyranose is the only common aldohexose that can place all of its bulky substituents equatorial in a single chair, which is exactly why the conformation shown is the favored one; ring-flipping to the alternate chair would force every one of those groups axial and is strongly disfavored.

(Choice C) is correct: the outward-angled bulky groups occupy equatorial positions, and equatorial placement of bulky groups minimizes steric strain, which is what stabilizes the conformation shown.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the geometry. The bulky groups in the favored chair are equatorial, not axial, and axial bulky groups actually suffer 1,3-diaxial steric clashes. There is no stabilizing "electron-cloud overlap" between them. That description fits the disfavored, all-axial ring-flipped chair, not the one shown.

(Choice A) is incorrect: the ring oxygen does not lock the substituents in place. Cyclic sugars freely interconvert between chair (and boat) conformations without breaking any covalent bonds; the shown chair predominates because it is lower in energy, not because interconversion is forbidden.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the favored form is a puckered chair, not a flat planar ring. A planar six-membered ring is a high-energy arrangement with eclipsing and angle strain; the whole point of the chair is to pucker out of the plane so substituents can adopt distinct axial and equatorial positions.

At the Skill 2 level, you cannot answer by recalling a definition. You must read the figure, recognize that the outward-angled groups are the equatorial ones, and then apply the principle that equatorial placement of bulky substituents reduces steric strain to explain why the shown conformation is preferred.$px$ WHERE id = 'f08ec5b2-76f0-485c-8330-dc1053c1e1de';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because a pre-equivalence point in a strong acid-strong base titration has its \(\text{pH}\) set by the leftover excess strong acid, and reaching it requires first finding the true equivalence volume from the mismatched concentrations and then diluting the excess over the total combined volume. First, the moles of acid: \(n_{\text{HCl}} = 0.06000\,\text{L} \times 0.100\,\text{M} = 6.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\). Because the titrant is three times as concentrated as the analyte, the equivalence volume is not half the acid volume; it is \(V_{\text{eq}} = \dfrac{6.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}}{0.300\,\text{M}} = 0.02000\,\text{L} = 20.0\,\text{mL}\). Three-quarters of the way to equivalence is \(V_{\text{NaOH}} = 0.75 \times 20.0\,\text{mL} = 15.0\,\text{mL}\), delivering \(n_{\text{NaOH}} = 0.300\,\text{M} \times 0.01500\,\text{L} = 4.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\). The added \(\text{OH}^-\) neutralizes an equal amount of \(\text{H}^+\), leaving excess \(\text{H}^+ = 6.00\times10^{-3} - 4.50\times10^{-3} = 1.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) (equivalently, one quarter of the original acid remains). The total volume is now \(60.0 + 15.0 = 75.0\,\text{mL} = 0.07500\,\text{L}\), so \([\text{H}^+] = \dfrac{1.50\times10^{-3}}{0.07500} = 0.0200\,\text{M}\) and \(\text{pH} = -\log(0.0200) = 1.70\). (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 1.22\) reverses the fraction, taking "three-quarters of the way" to mean three-quarters of the acid still remains; this assigns excess \(\text{H}^+ = 0.75 \times 6.00\times10^{-3} = 4.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\), which over the correct \(0.07500\,\text{L}\) gives \([\text{H}^+] = 0.0600\,\text{M}\); in fact three-quarters to equivalence means three-quarters of the acid has been neutralized, so only one quarter remains. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 1.60\) uses the correct excess of \(1.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) but divides by the original acid volume \(0.06000\,\text{L}\) to get \(0.0250\,\text{M}\); this ignores that the \(15.0\,\text{mL}\) of added titrant dilutes the solution, so the total volume \(0.07500\,\text{L}\) must be used. (Choice D) \(\text{pH} = 1.85\) drops the controlling concentration mismatch and treats the titrant as if it were the same concentration as the acid, so "three-quarters of the way" is taken as \(0.75 \times 60.0\,\text{mL} = 45.0\,\text{mL}\) of titrant and the total volume as \(105.0\,\text{mL}\); dividing the excess \(1.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) by \(0.10500\,\text{L}\) gives \(0.0143\,\text{M}\), but the \(0.300\,\text{M}\) titrant reaches equivalence at only \(20.0\,\text{mL}\), so the volume actually added is \(15.0\,\text{mL}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the equivalence-volume relationship under unequal concentrations with the dilution of excess strong acid, chaining several intermediate quantities to reach the pH.$px$ WHERE id = 'f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is A because the stem gives ACC's regulation and the fasting cell's signal profile: glucagon-driven phosphorylation inhibits ACC, rising long-chain fatty acyl-CoA inhibits ACC as a feedback product, and the two activators (insulin and high citrate that mark the fed state) are absent. Every relevant signal points the same way, so ACC is shut down and de novo fatty-acid synthesis is largely off. That is appropriate for a fasting cell that should be burning, not building, fat.

(Choice A) is correct: fasting phosphorylation plus product inhibition by acyl-CoA turn ACC off while the activating fed-state signals are missing, so synthesis is suppressed.

(Choice B) is incorrect: low insulin is a fasting signal that removes activation and permits inhibitory phosphorylation; it does not 'remove a brake' to speed synthesis up.

(Choice C) is incorrect: long-chain fatty acyl-CoA is the pathway's downstream product and a feedback inhibitor of ACC, not its substrate (ACC's substrate is acetyl-CoA); its accumulation slows synthesis.

(Choice D) is incorrect: ACC integrates several inputs (insulin/glucagon phosphorylation and acyl-CoA feedback, not citrate alone), and the given fasting signals are enough to predict that synthesis is off.

This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate multiple regulatory inputs to predict whether the pathway is active in a defined physiological state.$px$ WHERE id = 'f181719d-398a-4428-a315-21cf06c9d142';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is A because the hallmark event that defines S (synthesis) phase is DNA replication, which produces identical paired DNA molecules called sister chromatids joined at the centromere. During S phase, DNA replication proceeds to form identical pairs of DNA molecules (sister chromatids) that remain firmly attached at the centromeric region, and the centrosome is also duplicated during this phase. The other listed events belong to different stages of the cell cycle: the gap phases (G1 and G2) prepare for and follow replication, and chromosome segregation occurs in the mitotic phase, not interphase. Recognizing which molecular event names S phase therefore depends on assigning DNA replication specifically to this stage. (Choice A) is correct because replicating DNA into centromere-linked sister chromatids is the defining event of S phase. (Choice B) describes the G1 phase, during which the cell accumulates the building blocks of chromosomal DNA as well as sufficient energy reserves to complete the task of replicating each chromosome, not S phase itself. (Choice C) describes the G2 phase, in which the cell synthesizes proteins necessary for chromosome manipulation and movement and the cytoskeleton is dismantled to provide resources for the mitotic phase. (Choice D) describes the mitotic phase, during which the replicated DNA and cytoplasmic contents are separated and the duplicated chromosomes are segregated, which follows interphase rather than occurring within S phase. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling and correctly assigning the defining biochemical event (DNA replication into sister chromatids) to the S phase of the cell cycle.$px$ WHERE id = 'f1b986be-e396-4c86-9870-728ebf129f2a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential.' The falling phase of an action potential is produced by passive K+ efflux through voltage-gated K+ channels, and the brief hyperpolarizing overshoot arises because those channels are delayed in closing. The text notes that as K+ leaves the cell "the membrane potential begins to move back toward its resting voltage. This is called repolarization," and that "those K+ channels are slightly delayed in closing, accounting for this short overshoot." The Na+/K+ ATPase does not shape any single spike; its job is slow restoration, since "after the repolarizing phase of the action potential, K+ leakage channels and the Na+/K+ pump ensure that the ions return to their original locations." Because these ionic gradients change only slightly per spike, one action potential fires normally even with the pump blocked, but without the pump those dissipated gradients cannot be rebuilt, so sustained high-rate firing runs them down. (Choice A) is incorrect: the final part of the falling phase and the overshoot are driven by continued K+ efflux, not by the pump's small electrogenic current, so blocking the pump does not truncate repolarization or erase the overshoot of a single spike. (Choice B) is incorrect: repolarization does not require removing the Na+ that entered. Na+ entry is halted by the voltage-gated Na+ channel's inactivation gate, and the fall is carried by K+ leaving, so the first spike repolarizes normally without the pump. (Choice C) is incorrect: leakage channels move ions down their gradients and thereby dissipate them; only the ATP-driven pump moves Na+ and K+ back against their gradients, so leak channels cannot substitute for it and repeated firing cannot be sustained indefinitely. (Choice D) is correct: the single spike's passive K+-driven falling phase and overshoot are unaffected, while only the pump-dependent restoration of the gradients, and thus sustained repeated firing, is lost. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the passive, channel-driven ionic basis of a single action potential and the active, pump-driven restoration of gradients to predict how a pump block differentially affects one spike versus repeated firing.$px$ WHERE id = 'f272f205-5d82-4ab5-9136-0e98530f0afd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' Retroviruses such as HIV carry an RNA genome that must be reverse transcribed into DNA before it can be integrated into the host chromosome. Reverse transcription never occurs in uninfected host cells, because reverse transcriptase is produced only from the expression of viral genes within an infected cell. Since HIV supplies some of its own enzymes that are not found in the host, drugs can be designed to inhibit these enzymes without affecting the host's metabolism. The observation of viral suppression with no host toxicity therefore points to an enzyme that belongs to the virus, not the cell. (Choice A) is correct: reverse transcriptase is a virus-encoded enzyme absent from uninfected host cells, so a drug that blocks it cripples viral replication while leaving normal host processes untouched. That is exactly the selectivity described. (Choice B) is incorrect: the RNA-to-DNA copying is not carried out by a host cell-cycle polymerase; if it were a host enzyme, blocking it would disrupt the patient's own dividing cells and cause the very toxicity the experiment shows is absent. (Choice C) is incorrect: the order is reversed. Reverse transcription produces the DNA copy first, and only that DNA is then integrated into the host genome, so the copying step cannot depend on prior integration. (Choice D) is incorrect: uninfected host cells do not routinely reverse-transcribe their own mRNA into DNA; this copying is a virus-specific activity, which is precisely why it can be targeted without harming the host. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that reverse transcriptase is a virus-supplied enzyme absent from normal cells to explain why inhibiting the RNA-to-DNA copying step selectively blocks the virus without host toxicity.$px$ WHERE id = 'f29988c8-09ce-46ae-87ec-2e2d75cd58c3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Each class of reversible inhibition leaves a distinct fingerprint on the two Michaelis-Menten parameters, so the class can be read off the pattern of change in \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\): in pure noncompetitive inhibition \(K_m\) remains unchanged, while both parameters change in mixed inhibition. Because the inhibitor here binds free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex with equal affinity, it lowers the apparent \(V_{max}\) but does not perturb the E + S ⇌ ES equilibrium, so \(K_m\) stays the same and the Lineweaver-Burk lines share a common x-intercept. (Choice A) is correct: a decreased \(V_{max}\) with an unchanged \(K_m\) (lines intersecting on the x-axis) is the hallmark of noncompetitive inhibition, in which the inhibitor binds to both E and ES with equal (Kis = Kii) affinity, so the apparent \(V_m\) is less than the real \(V_m\) while the apparent \(K_m\) does not change. (Choice B) is incorrect: competitive inhibitors bind the same site as substrate in a mutually exclusive fashion, so excess substrate outcompetes them; \(V_{max}\) is unchanged while \(K_m\) increases. That is the opposite of the unchanged \(K_m\) and lowered \(V_{max}\) observed here. (Choice C) is incorrect: uncompetitive inhibitors bind only to ES and not free E, which lowers both the apparent \(K_m\) and \(V_{max}\) together; here \(K_m\) does not fall, so the data exclude this class. (Choice D) is incorrect: mixed inhibitors bind E and ES with different affinities (Kis ≠ Kii), so both parameters change: \(V_{max}\) falls and \(K_m\) shifts. An unchanged \(K_m\) rules out mixed inhibition and points specifically to the pure noncompetitive case. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic effects of each inhibition class on \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) to the reported data and deduce that an unchanged \(K_m\) with a lowered \(V_{max}\) identifies noncompetitive inhibition.$px$ WHERE id = 'f3512c92-730c-409b-8c6c-59ee3300dd8e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' Forming \(\text{XF}_6\) requires the central atom to hold six bonding pairs (twelve electrons), an expanded octet. Second-period elements (\(n=2\)) have only four valence orbitals (one \(2s\) and three \(2p\)) and can therefore accommodate a maximum of eight valence electrons, so they cannot be hypervalent; elements in the third period and beyond (\(n\ge 3\)) have additional accessible orbitals and can share more than four pairs. (Choice A) is correct: oxygen is a second-period element limited to four valence orbitals and eight electrons, so it cannot expand its octet to hold the twelve electrons an \(\text{XF}_6\) molecule requires. No stable neutral \(\text{OF}_6\) forms. (Choice B) is incorrect: selenium sits directly below sulfur in the fourth period, has orbitals available beyond an octet, and forms \(\text{SeF}_6\), so it can serve as the central atom. (Choice C) is incorrect: tellurium is a fifth-period element that likewise exceeds an octet and forms \(\text{TeF}_6\), so a stable molecule is expected. (Choice D) is incorrect: even though xenon is a noble gas, it is a fifth-period element with orbitals to expand its valence shell and forms hypervalent fluorides such as \(\text{XeF}_6\), so it can be the central atom. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that only elements in period 3 or higher can expand beyond an octet to a set of candidate central atoms, distinguishing them by period rather than by counting the electrons the stem already supplies.$px$ WHERE id = 'f38747a2-57a2-4094-aa0e-3bf37573f408';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'

The answer is A because the elevated H+ (lower pH) and elevated CO2 in metabolically active muscle are heterotropic allosteric effectors that stabilize the T (deoxy) state of hemoglobin, lowering its affinity for O2. Lower affinity shifts the oxygen-dissociation curve to the right, which raises \(P_{50}\) (the pO2 at half-saturation, a higher number meaning it takes more O2 pressure to half-saturate). A right-shifted curve means that at the same tissue pO2, hemoglobin holds a smaller fraction of its O2, so it unloads more O2 exactly where the sprinting muscle needs it. This is the Bohr effect operating to match O2 delivery to demand.

(Choice A) is correct: right shift, higher \(P_{50}\), and enhanced unloading are the coupled consequences of decreased affinity produced by the local acidosis and hypercapnia.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the relationship. A leftward shift with a lower \(P_{50}\) is what increased affinity looks like (e.g., in the lungs as O2 loads and H+/CO2 dissociate). Low pH and high CO2 in active tissue do the opposite: they decrease affinity, not increase it.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly names the rightward shift and rising \(P_{50}\) but attaches the wrong functional outcome. A right shift by definition lowers affinity, so hemoglobin releases more O2, not less; "holds O2 more tightly" contradicts the very shift being described.

(Choice D) is incorrect: hemoglobin's affinity is pH- and CO2-dependent (unlike myoglobin), so these effectors modulate the curve within the tissue capillary, not only in the lungs. The fall in tissue pO2 does drive unloading, but the Bohr effect additionally right-shifts the curve so that more O2 is released for the same pO2 drop.

Skill 2 note: succeeding here requires reasoning from the given local conditions (falling pH, rising CO2) to a direction of curve shift, then linking that shift to its physiological consequence for oxygen delivery, not recalling a stated definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'f3c6bbd8-43dd-4c85-b669-a05e9f998727';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is B because the synthetase cleaves ATP all the way to AMP + \(\text{PP}_i\), not to ADP + \(\text{P}_i\). Regenerating ATP from AMP requires rebuilding two phosphoanhydride bonds (AMP → ADP → ATP), so the single activation step has drawn down the cell's high-energy phosphate pool by two ATP equivalents even though only one ATP molecule was hydrolyzed.

(Choice B) is correct: because the bond broken releases AMP + \(\text{PP}_i\), two high-energy bonds are spent, and it takes two phosphorylation steps to remake ATP, so the cost is counted as "2 ATP equivalents."
(Choice A) is incorrect: carnitine loading (the CPT-I step) does not consume ATP; the acyl group is simply transesterified from CoA onto carnitine, so there is no second ATP hydrolysis here.
(Choice C) is incorrect: activation does not yield ADP + \(\text{P}_i\); it yields AMP + \(\text{PP}_i\). Treating the products as ADP + \(\text{P}_i\) would wrongly imply only one high-energy bond was used.
(Choice D) is incorrect: the \(\text{FADH}_2\) and \(\text{NADH}\) generated during β-oxidation are energy-yielding products, not costs; they are not why activation is charged as two ATP.

This item rewards Skill 2 reasoning: rather than recalling a stated ATP tally, you must trace which bonds of ATP are broken and infer the true energetic cost of restoring the nucleotide pool.$px$ WHERE id = 'f3dfe30f-212b-4175-8d4c-2ac6fc0c71a1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC).' An oxidizing agent (oxidant) is the substance that brings about the oxidation of another species and, in doing so, is itself reduced. Its oxidation number decreases. To find it, assign oxidation numbers and locate the reactant whose number drops. Here \(\text{Fe}\) goes from 0 in the free metal to +2 in \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) (oxidized, oxidation number increases), while the copper in \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) goes from +2 to 0 in \(\text{Cu(s)}\) (reduced, oxidation number decreases). (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\) is the reactant species whose oxidation number decreases from +2 to 0, so it is reduced; because it accepts the electrons lost by iron and thereby oxidizes the iron, \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is the oxidizing agent. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{Fe(s)}\) is oxidized (0 to +2), meaning it loses electrons and brings about the reduction of copper; a species that is itself oxidized is the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent. This is the reversed-role trap. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Cu(s)}\) is the reduced product, the outcome of \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) gaining electrons; the oxidizing agent is the reactant that does the oxidizing, not the elemental product that is formed after reduction is complete. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\) is the oxidized product formed when iron loses electrons; it is a product of oxidation rather than a reactant that drives it, so it cannot be the oxidizing agent in this forward reaction. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply oxidation-number assignments to identify which reactant is reduced and match it to the definition of an oxidizing agent.$px$ WHERE id = 'f441bb96-f246-4989-88d9-6ee3cf53f0b5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is B because at any pH greater than the isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\)), an amino acid carries a net negative charge, and pH 9.0 is well above alanine's \(\text{pI}\) of 6.0. The \(\text{pI}\) is the pH of zero net charge; raising the pH above it deprotonates ionizable groups, removing positive charge and exposing negative charge. At pH 9.0 the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\) 2.34) is essentially fully deprotonated (\(\text{-COO}^-\), charge \(-1\)), while the \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a\) 9.69) is partially protonated: since pH 9.0 is below 9.69, slightly more than half of the amino groups still carry \(\text{-NH}_3^+\). Combining a full \(-1\) from the carboxylate with a fractional positive (between \(0\) and \(+1\)) from the amino group yields a net charge between \(-1\) and \(0\), a net negative value. The qualitative rule (pH > pI gives net negative) is sufficient and matches the arithmetic. (Choice A) At pH 9.0 the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a\) 2.34) is far above its \(\text{pK}_a\) and is therefore deprotonated, not protonated, so the claim that both groups remain protonated is incorrect and would not produce a net positive charge. (Choice C) Net zero charge occurs only at the \(\text{pI}\) (~6.0), not at pH 9.0; the existence of a local buffering region near a \(\text{pK}_a\) does not make the net charge zero. (Choice D) This reverses the relationship: raising the pH above the \(\text{pI}\) drives deprotonation (loss of \(\text{H}^+\)), making the molecule more negative, not more positive. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = 'f498bc70-a7a9-4062-9fdc-12662ec3d259';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' For the binary hydrogen halides of group 17, acid strength in water increases down the group, HF < HCl < HBr < HI, because the H-A bond strength decreases as the halogen gets larger; the weaker the H-A bond, the more readily the molecule ionizes to release \(\text{H}^+\). Fluorine is the smallest halogen and forms an unusually short, strong H-F bond, so HF alone is a weak acid while the others are strong acids. This trend is governed by bond strength, not by electronegativity, which is the key distinction being tested. (Choice A) is correct: the strong H-F bond is difficult to break, so only a small fraction of HF molecules ionize in water, making it a weak acid. (Choice B) is incorrect: it treats electronegativity as the controlling factor for the down-group trend, but greater bond polarity does not make the H-F bond easier to break; the strong H-F bond actually resists ionization, which is why HF is the weakest, not the strongest, acid. (Choice C) is incorrect: it invokes a real property (fluorine is the smallest halogen) but reaches the wrong conclusion; the small, highly charge-dense \(\text{F}^-\) ion is strongly hydrated and paired with the strong H-F bond, and the net result is weak, not enhanced, ionization. (Choice D) is incorrect: fluorine has seven valence electrons and completes its octet by sharing one pair with hydrogen, so HF is a normal closed-shell molecule with no incomplete octet; its weak acidity comes from bond strength, not an electronic deficiency. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the group 17 trend that binary hydrogen-halide acid strength is governed by decreasing H-A bond strength down the group, rather than by electronegativity, to explain the anomalous weakness of HF.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"The exceptionally strong H-F bond resists ionization, so relatively little HF dissociates to release \\(\\text{H}^+\\) in water."},{"label":"B","text":"Fluorine's high electronegativity pulls the shared pair so strongly that the H-F bond ionizes more readily than any other hydrogen halide."},{"label":"C","text":"Because fluorine is the smallest halogen, the compact \\(\\text{F}^-\\) ion is a very stable conjugate base that drives ionization nearly to completion."},{"label":"D","text":"Fluorine's single unpaired valence electron leaves HF with an incomplete octet, preventing it from donating a proton to water."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'f4f7b834-bb1f-401a-b380-2f173274871c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is C because five days without food is well past glycogen depletion, so blood glucose now comes from gluconeogenesis using glycerol and amino acids, and by this point the brain is using ketone bodies extensively. That lowers glucose demand and therefore spares body protein.

(Choice A) is incorrect: hepatic glycogen is essentially gone within about a day, so glycogenolysis cannot be the main source at day five.

(Choice B) is incorrect: this describes the absorptive/early phase; by day five dietary and glycogen sources are long exhausted and ketones are abundant, so it misplaces an early stage onto a late one.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly names gluconeogenesis but denies the ketone adaptation that is precisely what curbs glucose demand and protects protein at this stage; in fact protein breakdown is slowing, not accelerating, by day five.

(Choice C) is correct: it pairs the correct late-fast glucose source with the ketone-driven, protein-sparing mechanism that defines prolonged starvation.

This item rewards Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving): you must place a specified point in the fasting timeline and reason out both the dominant glucose source and the sparing mechanism, not recall a single fact.$px$ WHERE id = 'f570021f-6365-4a11-94f2-34e94bb3bdb1';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A chemist examines the anomeric carbon of a sugar residue and finds that it is bonded to two oxygen atoms, each of which is part of a separate carbon-oxygen-carbon bridge; the carbon carries no directly attached hydroxyl group. When this residue is warmed with a mild cupric oxidizing solution that reacts only with free aldehydes, no brick-red precipitate forms. Which conclusion about the anomeric carbon best accounts for both the bonding described and the negative test result?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because the described anomeric carbon has two separate C-O-C bridges (two \(-\text{OR}\) groups) and no free \(-\text{OH}\), which is the defining bonding pattern of an acetal, i.e., a glycoside. In the cyclic form of a monosaccharide, the anomeric carbon must retain one free \(-\text{OH}\) (a hemiacetal) to reversibly ring-open into the reactive open-chain aldehyde. Replacing that free \(-\text{OH}\) with a second \(-\text{OR}\) converts the hemiacetal into a full acetal, which cannot ring-open to an aldehyde. With no free aldehyde available, the mild cupric reagent has nothing to oxidize, so no \(\text{Cu}_2\text{O}\) precipitate forms. The residue is non-reducing at that carbon. The bonding (two \(-\text{OR}\), no \(-\text{OH}\)) and the negative test are therefore two consequences of the same fact.

(Choice D) is correct: two \(-\text{OR}\) groups and no free \(-\text{OH}\) define an acetal/glycoside, which is locked closed and cannot open to an oxidizable aldehyde, explaining the negative result.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it misreads the bonding. The stem specifies no free \(-\text{OH}\) at the anomeric carbon, so this "single free -OH" premise contradicts the described acetal. A hemiacetal that could open would in fact give a positive test, not a negative one.

(Choice C) is incorrect: a hemiacetal has one \(-\text{OR}\) and one free \(-\text{OH}\), not the two \(-\text{OR}\) groups described, so the residue is not a hemiacetal; invoking a "ketone form" also does not follow from the bonding given.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it self-contradicts by claiming two \(-\text{OR}\) groups yet still a free \(-\text{OH}\); an acetal carbon has no free \(-\text{OH}\), and reducing behavior requires ring-opening to an aldehyde, not direct oxidation of a hydroxyl at the anomeric carbon.

This is a Skill 2 item because it asks you to apply the hemiacetal-versus-acetal bonding rule to a described anomeric carbon and predict its chemical behavior, rather than to recall a stated definition.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"Its two -OR linkages leave one free -OH, which is oxidized directly without any ring opening."},{"label":"B","text":"Its single free -OH allows it to open reversibly to an aldehyde, but the aldehyde is too hindered to be oxidized."},{"label":"C","text":"It is a hemiacetal, so the negative result must come from the ketone form resisting the mild oxidant."},{"label":"D","text":"Its two -OR linkages lock the ring closed, so it cannot revert to an open-chain aldehyde and act as a reducing group."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'f5963226-2949-4291-adcc-781e5cdbbc86';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is D because a clean \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) dihybrid \(F_2\) ratio is the diagnostic signature that the two genes sorted into gametes independently of one another. Mendel's law of independent assortment states that genes do not influence each other with regard to the sorting of alleles into gametes, so that a gamete receiving an \(r\) allele is equally likely to receive a \(Y\) or a \(y\) allele; this produces four equally likely gametes (\(YR\), \(Yr\), \(yR\), \(yr\)) and a 16-box Punnett square whose phenotypes fall in the \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) pattern. The recovery of all four phenotypic classes in exactly these proportions confirms that the texture and color genes assorted as independent events. (Choice A) Linked genes are located physically close on the same chromosome and tend to be transmitted together, so their alleles do NOT assort freely; because linkage would mean the classical Mendelian \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) prediction for a dihybrid cross does not apply, the observed ratio rules linkage out rather than demonstrating it. (Choice B) Epistasis is a two-gene interaction in which one gene masks the other, and although such crosses still total 16 parts, they yield modified ratios such as \(9{:}7\), \(12{:}3{:}1\), or \(15{:}1\). None of those is the unmodified \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) seen here. (Choice C) The law of segregation correctly describes that each gene contributes one allele per gamete and underlies each individual 3:1 monohybrid split, but segregation alone does not specify how the two genes combine; the dihybrid \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) pattern specifically requires the second law, independent assortment, layering the two 3:1 ratios together. (Choice D) is correct: the \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) ratio collapses into two independent 3:1 ratios (3 round:1 wrinkled and 3 yellow:1 green), and applying the product rule \((3/4)\times(3/4)=9/16\) reproduces the dihybrid proportions, which is the hallmark of independent assortment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the law of independent assortment to reason from an observed dihybrid phenotypic ratio back to the underlying inheritance principle.$px$ WHERE id = 'f59ac918-f00d-47a3-b1dc-c8c72bbc7050';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization.' A reflex arc is built from a set of functional neuron classes: sensory (afferent) neurons carry input from receptors into the CNS, motor (efferent) neurons carry output from the CNS to effectors, and interneurons sit entirely within the CNS as the integrating center that connects the afferent and efferent branches. A reflex arc is a circuit that couples a sensory input to a motor output through an integrating center linking the two branches; a monosynaptic reflex is one in which the sensory neuron activates the motor neuron directly, whereas a polysynaptic reflex such as the hot-stove withdrawal interposes an interneuron between them. The knee-jerk stretch reflex is monosynaptic: the muscle-spindle sensory neuron drives contraction of the muscle directly by synapsing straight onto the motor neuron, so no integrating relay participates in the direct excitatory limb. (Choice A) is incorrect: the sensory (afferent) neuron is present. It is the muscle-spindle neuron that detects the stretch and carries that input into the spinal cord, forming the first neuron of the two-neuron pathway. (Choice B) is incorrect: the motor (efferent) neuron is present. It is the second neuron whose axon leaves the cord to contract the quadriceps, so removing it would eliminate the contraction the stem describes. (Choice C) is correct: because the sensory neuron synapses directly onto the motor neuron, the arc is monosynaptic and contains no interneuron in the excitatory pathway; the interneuron is the integrating class that would only appear if a relay were interposed between the afferent and efferent branches, as in a polysynaptic reflex. (Choice D) is incorrect: the postganglionic autonomic neuron is not one of the reflex arc's functional classes at all. It belongs to the visceral (autonomic) efferent pathway that drives smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands; the somatic knee-jerk reflex contracts skeletal muscle and never includes an autonomic neuron regardless of synapse count. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional classification of reflex-arc neurons and the monosynaptic-versus-polysynaptic distinction to infer, from the stated direct synapse, which neuron class is missing from the arc.$px$ WHERE id = 'f62255c8-f053-4943-87e1-f909f8decb25';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because the trachea carries the entire tidal volume through one narrow tube, forcing air to a high linear velocity; high velocity drives the flow into the turbulent regime (high Reynolds number), and turbulence generates the audible rushing breath sounds heard centrally. As the airway tree branches, the summed cross-sectional area of all the distal passages becomes enormous, so by continuity the linear velocity of the air falls dramatically. At these low velocities flow stays smooth and laminar (low Reynolds number), which is silent, so peripheral auscultation is nearly quiet. (Choice C) is correct: it correctly pairs high central velocity with turbulence/sound and low distal velocity with laminar/silent flow. (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the velocity relationship. The trachea has the smallest, not largest, total cross-sectional area, so central velocity is highest, not lowest, and central flow is turbulent, not laminar. (Choice B) is incorrect: sound is not generated by flow smoothness being 'absorbed' by gas exchange; silence distally results from laminar flow at low velocity, and audible sound centrally results from turbulence, independent of where diffusion occurs. (Choice D) is incorrect: breath sounds arise from turbulent airflow, not from tracheal cartilage vibrating, and cartilage does not determine whether flow is laminar or turbulent. This question assesses the skill of reasoning about how airway geometry sets flow velocity and thereby whether airflow is turbulent (audible) or laminar (silent).$px$ WHERE id = 'f62bfd57-df70-49a2-b3d9-9d8be39fb68e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the driving constraint inverts the ordinary galvanic rule. Left alone, the spontaneous (galvanic) cell would put the couple with the more positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) at the cathode: \(\text{Br}_2/\text{Br}^-\ (+1.07\ \text{V})\) would be reduced and \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\ (+0.34\ \text{V})\) would be oxidized, giving \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 1.07 - 0.34 = +0.73\ \text{V} > 0\). However, the external power source is adjusted to deposit copper, i.e., to force \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\) (reduction) at the cathode. By elimination, oxidation must occur at the other electrode, so \(2\text{Br}^- \rightarrow \text{Br}_2 + 2e^-\) is driven at the anode, even though \(\text{Br}_2/\text{Br}^-\) is the more positive (more easily reduced) couple. For this forced configuration, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = E^{\circ}(\text{Cu}) - E^{\circ}(\text{Br}) = 0.34 - 1.07 = -0.73\ \text{V} < 0\), so \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\): the operation is non-spontaneous (electrolytic) and requires an external voltage of at least \(0.73\ \text{V}\) to drive it. (Choice A) This blindly applies the galvanic rule (less positive couple is oxidized) and reports the spontaneous \(+0.73\ \text{V}\) cell, but the stated constraint deposits copper, forcing \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) to be reduced (not \(\text{Cu}\) oxidized), so this describes the cell running backward from what the power source enforces. (Choice C) This calls a reduction (\(\text{Br}_2 \rightarrow \text{Br}^-\)) an anode process, which is a contradiction because oxidation, by definition, occurs at the anode; it also wrongly concludes the driven cell is spontaneous. (Choice D) Depositing copper, \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\), is a reduction (gain of electrons) occurring at the cathode, not an oxidation at the anode, and \(+1.07\ \text{V}\) is merely a tabulated reduction potential, not the cell potential of this configuration. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = 'f674b557-eeec-4148-b8ae-a2e1f81a9679';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because the brain and red blood cells take up glucose through insulin-independent transporters (GLUT1/GLUT3), so even with almost no insulin they keep drawing glucose; and because red blood cells have no mitochondria, they can only run glycolysis, metabolizing glucose anaerobically to lactate in every metabolic state.

(Choice B) is correct: both tissues take up glucose without insulin, and mitochondria-free red blood cells are obligate anaerobic glucose users regardless of state.

(Choice A) is incorrect: brain and red blood cells do not rely on insulin-stimulated GLUT4 (that is muscle and adipose), so low insulin does not shut off their glucose uptake.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses their metabolism. Red blood cells cannot oxidize glucose to CO2 because they lack mitochondria, whereas the brain fully oxidizes glucose.

(Choice D) is incorrect: red blood cells and neurons cannot oxidize fatty acids (fatty acids do not effectively cross the blood-brain barrier and red cells lack mitochondria), so they do not switch to fat when insulin falls.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by making you reason from tissue architecture (transporters and mitochondria) to fuel use under low insulin.$px$ WHERE id = 'f706666d-2d49-48ad-baeb-709f73228178';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is A because the binding of a new \(\text{ATP}\) molecule to the myosin head is the event that causes the cross-bridge to detach from actin. ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from actin, and in the absence of ATP the myosin head cannot detach. This is exactly why rigor mortis occurs: with no further ATP production possible, there is no ATP available to detach the myosin heads from the actin-binding sites, so the cross-bridges stay in place and the skeletal muscles become rigid. Detachment requires \(\text{ATP}\) to occupy the myosin head's separate ATP-binding site, which weakens its hold on actin. (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{ATP}\) binding directly weakens the actin-myosin link and releases the cross-bridge, so its absence after death keeps the heads bound. (Choice B) is wrong because release of \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) does the opposite of detachment. It causes myosin to form a stronger attachment to the actin and initiates the power stroke, not release. (Choice C) is wrong because after the power stroke \(\text{ADP}\) is released, but the formed cross-bridge is still in place and actin and myosin remain bound together, so \(\text{ADP}\) release leaves the head attached. (Choice D) is wrong because hydrolysis of \(\text{ATP}\) to \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) occurs only after detachment and re-cocks the head into the high-energy position (the energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the myosin head into a cocked position), but it does not itself separate the head from actin. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that ATP binding, not phosphate or ADP release, is the trigger that detaches the myosin cross-bridge from actin.$px$ WHERE id = 'f70ccfd9-a1c6-4f80-b2d0-9c797a9bb99c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the chromium oxidation state decreases from \(+6\) to \(+3\), which is a gain of three electrons per chromium atom and therefore a reduction. (In \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\), each oxygen is \(-2\) and the overall charge is \(-2\), so \(2(\text{Cr}) + 7(-2) = -2\), giving \(\text{Cr} = +6\); in \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\) the oxidation state is \(+3\).) The species that is reduced is, by definition, the oxidizing agent. It accepts electrons and thereby oxidizes another species. Since the chromium in \(\text{K}_2\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7\) is reduced, \(\text{K}_2\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7\) is the oxidizing agent. (Choice B) This both mislabels the agent and misidentifies the direction of change: a drop from \(+6\) to \(+3\) is a decrease in oxidation number, so the chromium is reduced, not oxidized, and the species is therefore not the reducing agent. (Choice C) The direction of electron transfer is correctly identified as reduction, but the classification is reversed. The species that is reduced is the oxidizing agent, not the reducing agent. (Choice D) The classification as the oxidizing agent is correct, but the stated reason is wrong: a change from \(+6\) to \(+3\) is a gain of electrons (reduction), not oxidation. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = 'f74d3bde-0f70-4b67-b678-01354be96fd2';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A physiologist studies a healthy sea-level resident who ascends to a high-altitude research station, where the partial pressure of inspired O2 is markedly reduced. Over several days of acclimatization, glycolytic flux in the person's erythrocytes increases and the intracellular concentration of 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG) rises substantially above its sea-level value, while hemoglobin concentration and body temperature remain unchanged. The physiologist measures whole-blood oxygen-hemoglobin equilibrium curves before ascent and after acclimatization:

  • Before ascent: P50 = 26 mmHg
  • After acclimatization: P50 = 32 mmHg

In peripheral capillaries of actively respiring muscle, the local O2 partial pressure is about 20 to 30 mmHg. Compared with the sea-level condition, which single statement best describes how the acclimatized state affects hemoglobin's O2 affinity and the fraction of bound O2 that hemoglobin releases to this muscle tissue?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'

The answer is A because a rise in erythrocyte 2,3-BPG lowers hemoglobin's affinity for O2 and thereby increases O2 unloading at the tissues. \(P_{50}\) is the O2 partial pressure at which hemoglobin is half-saturated, so a higher \(P_{50}\) (26 → 32 mmHg) means more O2 is required to reach half-saturation: the curve is right-shifted and affinity is decreased. Mechanistically, 2,3-BPG is a strongly negatively charged allosteric effector that binds in the central cavity between the β subunits, where it forms additional salt bridges that stabilize the low-affinity T (tense, deoxy) state. Because affinity is lowest where it matters, at the low \(P_{O_2}\) of respiring muscle (about 20 to 30 mmHg), hemoglobin holds less tightly to O2 there and releases a larger fraction of its bound O2 to the tissue. This right-shift is the adaptive point of elevated 2,3-BPG at altitude: it improves peripheral O2 delivery.

(Choice A) is correct: decreased affinity (higher \(P_{50}\)) plus greater fractional unloading at low tissue \(P_{O_2}\) is exactly the physiological consequence of T-state stabilization by 2,3-BPG.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the direction of the affinity change. A higher \(P_{50}\) indicates lower, not higher, affinity, and a lower-affinity hemoglobin unloads more O2 at the tissue, not less. This choice treats 2,3-BPG as if it left-shifted the curve.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly reads the affinity change (decreased) but draws the wrong delivery conclusion. Decreased affinity means O2 is bound less tightly at low \(P_{O_2}\), so the fraction released to tissue rises, not falls; pairing "decreased affinity" with "less unloading" is internally inconsistent.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it inverts both physical facts, claiming increased affinity (which a rising \(P_{50}\) contradicts) yet more unloading. This mismatches the data, because an increased-affinity (left-shifted) hemoglobin would retain O2 more tightly and deliver less to tissue, the opposite of what is stated here.

Skill 2 note: this item requires interpreting \(P_{50}\) as an inverse index of affinity and then predicting a functional outcome (fractional O2 release at a specified low tissue \(P_{O_2}\)) from the effector's stabilization of the T state, rather than recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = 'f7e2ec85-859a-4980-b8d1-11ff97132acc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' A temperate bacteriophage can replicate by a lysogenic cycle in which the viral genome is inserted into the host-cell genome rather than immediately producing and releasing virions. When a temperate phage infects a bacterial cell by the lysogenic cycle, the viral genome is incorporated into the host-cell genome; the integrated phage DNA is then called a prophage, and because it is part of the host genome it is passed on to subsequent generations. (Choice A) is correct: as a prophage, the viral DNA is physically part of the bacterial chromosome, so it is duplicated by ordinary host DNA replication and each daughter cell inherits a copy without any new infection event. That explains why the genome spreads through a dividing population that survives and is never re-exposed to phage. (Choice B) is incorrect: this describes ongoing productive/lytic-style virion synthesis and conflates the prophage with a self-replicating plasmid; a lysogenic phage is silent and does not continuously make new particles, which is why no virion burst is seen. (Choice C) is incorrect: cell lysis to release virions is the lytic cycle, but the stem states that most cells survive and keep dividing, so widespread lysis and reinfection cannot be maintaining the genome. (Choice D) is incorrect: if host enzymes destroyed the phage genome it could not be present in nearly every descendant, and uptake of free DNA from the medium (transformation) is not how a prophage is propagated. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the lytic and lysogenic cycles to infer that only chromosomal integration (a prophage copied with the host DNA) can carry the viral genome through a surviving, dividing population.$px$ WHERE id = 'f81113a2-5cc1-45d2-812e-0ba013373fdc';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is A because the described phenotype (a deepened voice, increased muscle mass, and body hair) is the classic set of male secondary sex characteristics, and the gonadal steroid that principally drives their development is testosterone, secreted by the testes. (Choice A) is correct: testosterone is a steroid hormone whose effects include a deepened voice, increased muscle mass, and body hair, so it is the gonadal steroid responsible for the described male-pattern development. (Choice B) is incorrect: estrogens are the gonadal steroids that drive female secondary sex characteristics, such as development of breast and adipose tissue, rather than a deepened voice or increased muscle mass. (Choice C) is incorrect: progesterone is a gonadal steroid that helps regulate the menstrual cycle and prepares the body for pregnancy and childbirth, not the deepening of the voice or muscle growth described here. (Choice D) is incorrect: inhibin is a gonadal protein hormone that suppresses FSH release from the anterior pituitary and is not a steroid, so it does not produce secondary sex characteristics. Because this item asks the student to recognize which single gonadal sex steroid produces an explicitly described male phenotype, it tests foundational knowledge of hormone sources and effects (Skill 1).$px$ WHERE id = 'f897ae60-b855-42cb-a824-2263500656f6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' The vertebrate ectoderm gives rise to both the epidermis and neural tissue, and which fate a given region adopts is decided during neurulation by growth-factor signaling: these signals drive the cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis, while the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate. The critical, counterintuitive point is what the growth-factor signal actually does. It diverts cells toward the epidermal fate, so the default fate of ectoderm in the absence of that signal is neural. (Choice B) is correct: if growth-factor signaling were disrupted, the entire ectoderm would differentiate into neural tissue, because removing the signal removes the only cue that would have redirected the edge cells to epidermis. (Choice A) is incorrect: abolishing the signal does not swap the positions of the two fates; there is no residual signal to relocate the neural plate to the periphery, and the outcome is loss of the epidermal domain entirely, not a reversal. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the actual role of the signal. Students often assume the growth factor promotes the neural fate, but the signal drives the epidermal fate, so its loss expands neural tissue, not epidermis. (Choice D) is incorrect: the neural-versus-epidermal partition is not signal-independent; it depends entirely on the graded growth-factor signal, so eliminating that signal collapses the normal pattern rather than preserving it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the specific role of growth-factor signaling in ectodermal fate choice to predict the outcome of a novel perturbation, rather than recall the normal fate map.$px$ WHERE id = 'f8a331eb-4c06-43c9-be5b-9fdf6c78a2c1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is D because the ventral root carries only the motor (efferent) axons leaving the cord, so cutting it paralyzes the skeletal muscles of that segment, while the sensory (afferent) axons still reach the cord through the spared dorsal root, leaving skin sensation intact. As a spinal nerve nears the cord it splits into two roots: "The dorsal root contains only the axons of sensory neurons, whereas the ventral roots contain only the axons of the motor neurons," and "Motor neurons have cell bodies in the ventral gray matter of the spinal cord that project to muscle through the ventral root." Because the two roots are anatomically segregated, severing one selectively removes its single function while sparing the other. (Choice A) is reversed: the ventral root carries motor, not sensory, axons, so cutting it cannot abolish sensation while sparing movement. (Choice B) wrongly treats the root itself as mixed; although the spinal nerve "contains both sensory and motor axons," it splits into a purely sensory dorsal root and a purely motor ventral root, so a single root is not mixed. (Choice C) is only partly right, because the ventral root carries somatic motor axons in addition to autonomic ones (the sympathetic preganglionic axon "projects by way of the ventral spinal nerve root"), so somatic skeletal movement would also be lost, not preserved. (Choice D) correctly pairs motor paralysis with intact sensation, matching the exclusively motor content of the ventral root and the exclusively sensory content of the spared dorsal root. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the segregated dorsal-sensory/ventral-motor organization of spinal roots to predict the specific functional deficit produced by a defined anatomical lesion.$px$ WHERE id = 'f8c7fd2e-091f-4471-b093-8dfe475bb153';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because the bacterial flagellum is a rigid flagellin filament spun like a propeller by a rotary basal-body motor, whereas the sperm's bending stroke depends on a flexible 9+2 microtubule axoneme in which dynein motor arms slide adjacent doublets past each other; these are mechanistically different propulsion systems and the bacterial structure has none of the parts needed to bend. The prokaryotic flagellum is a stiff, rotating structure, whereas a eukaryotic flagellum behaves more like a flexible whip built from nine parallel pairs of microtubules surrounding a central pair; those microtubules use dynein motor proteins to move relative to one another, causing the flagellum to bend. Bacterial flagella are stiff spiral filaments of flagellin protein subunits that extend outward from the cell and spin in solution, driven by a basal body that acts as the motor for the flagellum. A stiff, rotating flagellin propeller therefore cannot execute the dynein-driven sliding-and-bending stroke used by the microtubule-based sperm flagellum. (Choice A) The bacterial flagellum DOES have a basal body (it is precisely the rotary motor embedded in the membrane), so claiming it lacks one is factually wrong; moreover, the real reason is the absence of a slideable microtubule axoneme, not a missing anchor. (Choice B) Correct: a stiff flagellin filament spun by a rotary motor is structurally incapable of the dynein-sliding bend, which is the key mechanistic contrast. (Choice C) The bacterial flagellum is built of flagellin protein, NOT a 9+2 microtubule array, and it contains no dynein; this imports eukaryotic axonemal architecture onto bacteria, two structurally distinct systems. (Choice D) Bacterial flagella have no dynein and do not bend at all (they rotate as rigid propellers), so the stroke is not "mechanistically identical"; this is the partial-truth trap that concedes the wrong mechanism. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must compare two propulsion systems and infer why the structural makeup of the bacterial flagellum rules out the eukaryotic bending mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = 'f8d7ec44-ac6f-4dc5-8804-e593751df999';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A three-carbon sugar in solution slowly interconverts between a form bearing a carbonyl at C2 and a transient intermediate in which C2 and C3 are joined by a double bond. A student tracking this interconversion labels the carbonyl oxygen and the two hydrogens on C3 with distinguishable isotopes. When the carbonyl form is converted into the double-bonded intermediate, which set of changes at the C2-C3 region is observed?

I. One hydrogen originally on C3 is no longer bonded to C3.
II. The oxygen at C2 gains a bond to hydrogen.
III. Both original C3 hydrogens remain bonded to C3, and a hydrogen is instead removed from C2.$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because the interconversion described is keto-enol tautomerization, the process that lets an open-chain sugar shift its carbonyl into a neighboring C=C double bond. Starting from the carbonyl form \(\text{C2}=\text{O}\) with a hydrogen-bearing C3 adjacent to it, forming the C2=C3 double bond requires two coordinated changes: a hydrogen must leave the carbon next to the carbonyl (here C3, the \(\alpha\)-carbon) so that carbon can donate electrons into the new \(\pi\) bond, and the carbonyl oxygen must gain a hydrogen so that \(\text{C2}=\text{O}\) becomes \(\text{C2}\text{-OH}\) (the enol/enediol hydroxyl). Both statement I (a C3 hydrogen is lost) and statement II (the oxygen gains a hydrogen) capture exactly these paired changes, so I and II together are correct.

(Choice A) is incorrect: II only is wrong because protonating the oxygen alone cannot create a carbon-carbon double bond; a hydrogen must also be removed from the adjacent carbon (statement I) to free up the electrons that form the C=C.

(Choice C) is incorrect: III only is wrong because it removes the hydrogen from the carbonyl carbon (C2) itself. C2 has no hydrogen to lose in a ketone, and even in an aldehyde the carbonyl carbon's electrons are already committed to oxygen; the hydrogen that leaves comes from the neighboring \(\alpha\)-carbon, not from the carbonyl carbon.

(Choice B) is correct: I and II only is correct: the \(\alpha\)-carbon (C3) loses a hydrogen and the carbonyl oxygen gains one, converting \(\text{C}=\text{O} + \text{C}\text{-H}\) into \(\text{C}\text{-OH} + \text{C}=\text{C}\).

(Choice D) is incorrect: I and III only is wrong because it double-counts hydrogen loss, stripping a hydrogen from both carbons. Only one hydrogen total is relocated to oxygen; if hydrogens were pulled from both carbons the atom count would not balance and no O-H would form.

Skill 2 is assessed here because you are given a described chemical transformation and must reason from the mechanism of tautomerization (tracking which specific bonds break and form) rather than recalling a memorized fact, applying the C=O + C-H ⇌ C-OH + C=C relationship to predict the labeled-atom outcome.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"II only, because the oxygen at C2 is protonated to give C2-OH while the carbon skeleton and every C-H bond in the region stay completely intact."},{"label":"B","text":"I and II only, because a hydrogen leaves C3 while the oxygen at C2 gains one as the C=C forms."},{"label":"C","text":"III only, because the carbon that carried the carbonyl must itself lose a hydrogen in order for the new C2=C3 double bond to form."},{"label":"D","text":"I and III only, because a hydrogen is stripped from both C2 and C3 at once to make room for the new double bond."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = 'f910061a-efbc-425a-9936-135a4978ccf1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because excitation signals delivered by a somatic motor neuron at the neuromuscular junction (NMJ) are the only way to functionally activate a skeletal muscle fiber to contract. Every skeletal muscle fiber is innervated by a motor neuron at the NMJ, and excitation signals from that neuron are the only means of functionally activating the fiber to contract. Unlike cardiac and smooth muscle, skeletal muscle depends completely on signaling from the nervous system to work properly, so the "excitation" step in skeletal muscle is always triggered by the somatic motor division of the nervous system. The voluntary movements skeletal muscle produces therefore require neural drive routed through the NMJ. (Choice A) A circulating hormone can trigger cardiac and smooth muscle, which are able to respond to other stimuli such as hormones and local signals, but skeletal muscle has no such hormonal activation pathway. (Choice B) Intrinsic rhythmic activity describes cardiomyocytes, which contract on their own intrinsic rhythms without any external stimulation, a property skeletal muscle fibers lack. (Choice C) Mechanical stretch can trigger smooth muscle (the stress-relaxation response), but stretch does not functionally activate a skeletal muscle fiber, which still requires neural excitation. (Choice D) A somatic motor neuron firing at the NMJ is correct because this is the sole functional route to skeletal muscle contraction; without this neural excitation the fiber cannot contract regardless of other conditions in the bath. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that skeletal muscle is voluntary and depends exclusively on somatic motor neuron excitation at the NMJ for activation.$px$ WHERE id = 'f9864f4d-86cc-4f77-9b1e-3a6f3334d9ab';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' In F-plasmid conjugation, the F pilus draws an F+ donor and an F− recipient together, a cytoplasmic bridge forms, and rolling-circle replication feeds a single strand of the F plasmid into the recipient, which then makes it double-stranded. Because rolling-circle replication leaves an intact copy of the plasmid behind, the donor stays F+ while the recipient is newly converted to F+. (Choice A) is correct: once the transferred strand is made double-stranded, the former F− cell becomes an F+ cell capable of building its own conjugation pilus, so in a mixed population the process spreads until essentially all cells are F+. The former recipients are themselves now donors that can pass the plasmid onward. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reflects the common misconception that transfer means giving the plasmid away; in fact rolling-circle replication copies the plasmid, so the donor retains its F plasmid and remains F+. (Choice C) is incorrect: typical conjugation in E. coli transfers the F-plasmid DNA only. Chromosomal genes (like the resistance gene) are co-transferred only when the F plasmid integrates into the chromosome to form an Hfr cell, which the stem explicitly excludes. (Choice D) is incorrect: bacterial conjugation is not eukaryotic sexual reproduction; ordinary F+ transfer moves a plasmid, not paired chromosomes, so no chromosomal recombination or new hybrid genotype is generated in the recipient. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of rolling-circle F-plasmid transfer to predict the genetic state of the recipient population, rather than recall the name of an appendage.$px$ WHERE id = 'f9afe4f4-d13f-494b-b88f-1e648857cf9e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is A because an uncoupler separates electron transport from ATP synthesis. Making the membrane leaky to protons collapses the gradient, so ATP synthase has little proton-motive force to work with and ATP output falls. But the pumps themselves are not blocked. In fact, with the gradient continually dissipated there is less backpressure, so electron transport and \(\text{O}_2\) consumption continue and can even speed up, and the energy that would have made ATP is released as heat.

(Choice A) is correct: uncoupling keeps electrons flowing to \(\text{O}_2\) while diverting the free energy into heat instead of ATP.

(Choice B) is incorrect: protons can still be pumped (the leak is a return path, not a block on the pumps), so electron transport and \(\text{O}_2\) consumption do not stop.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the whole point of an uncoupler is that the gradient IS dissipated; if the gradient were unaffected, ATP synthesis would proceed normally and no extra heat would be generated.

(Choice D) is incorrect: leaked protons bypass ATP synthase rather than being routed into it, so ATP synthesis falls, not rises, and \(\text{O}_2\) consumption increases rather than falling.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must reason that dissipating the gradient uncouples respiration from phosphorylation, sustaining \(\text{O}_2\) use while producing heat instead of ATP.$px$ WHERE id = 'f9c5eef1-c00c-4360-bfb1-b4b4466b3a56';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because an enzyme's substrate specificity comes from the amino acid residues that line its substrate-binding pocket, whose size, shape, and charge determine which side chain can dock. For serine proteases, this specificity is set by how the size, shape, and charge of the substrate's side chain fit into the enzyme's S1 binding pocket, and variations in the residues within that pocket enable electrostatic interactions with the substrate that dictate sequence specificity. Because trypsin's S1 pocket contains an aspartate that attracts basic side chains while chymotrypsin's pocket is large and hydrophobic and accommodates aromatic side chains, the two homologous enzymes recognize different substrates. (Choice A) is incorrect: lowering the activation energy for bound transition states is a property shared by all enzymes, so it cannot explain why these two enzymes select different substrates. (Choice B) is incorrect: both enzymes catalyze the same chemistry, peptide-bond hydrolysis, and an enzyme does not affect the thermodynamics of a reaction, so the reaction's free-energy change is neither set by the enzyme nor the source of its specificity. (Choice C) is incorrect: temperature changes globally affect reaction rate and denaturation (moderate increases in temperature typically enhance reaction rates, whereas excessive heat leads to irreversible denaturation), but they do not dictate which side chain a protease recognizes. (Choice D) is correct: the identity of the residues forming the binding pocket sets the size/shape/charge complementarity that determines substrate specificity. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that an enzyme's substrate specificity is set by the residues lining its binding pocket.$px$ WHERE id = 'fa662802-49bd-4151-8821-a912c7f21f19';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Skeletal muscle contains three fiber types distinguished by contractile speed and how they make ATP: slow oxidative (SO) fibers contract slowly and resist fatigue, fast oxidative (FO) fibers contract quickly yet stay relatively fatigue-resistant, and fast glycolytic (FG) fibers contract quickly but fatigue rapidly. The described muscle powers repeated jogging while resisting fatigue better than sprint fibers, identifying it as FO-dominant; the sprinter's explosive-push fibers are FG. FO fibers are oxidative because they produce ATP aerobically, contain high amounts of mitochondria, and do not fatigue quickly, whereas FG fibers rely primarily on anaerobic glycolysis and do not possess substantial numbers of mitochondria. (Choice A) is incorrect: FO fibers do have more mitochondria than FG fibers, but they do not merely match FG fibers in metabolism. They rely far more on aerobic respiration, so "similar reliance" understates the metabolic difference. (Choice B) is correct: relative to FG fibers, FO fibers possess both more mitochondria and a greater reliance on aerobic (oxidative) respiration, which is what makes them fast yet fatigue-resistant. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the relationship. It describes the FG (sprint) fibers themselves, which have fewer mitochondria and depend on anaerobic glycolysis, not the more fatigue-resistant FO fibers. (Choice D) is incorrect: it keeps the glycolytic metabolism of sprint fibers while wrongly claiming fewer mitochondria than an already low-mitochondria fiber, describing no real fiber type and contradicting the aerobic basis of FO fatigue resistance. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how mitochondrial content and aerobic versus anaerobic ATP production distinguish fast oxidative from fast glycolytic muscle fibers.$px$ WHERE id = 'fafa1030-f2f2-4ca4-bd30-816985003d0b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because the blood entering the alveolar capillaries has a low PO2 (40 mmHg) and a high PCO2 (45 mmHg), while alveolar air has a high PO2 (100 mmHg) and a lower PCO2 (40 mmHg); each gas diffuses independently down its own partial-pressure gradient, so O2 moves from the alveolus into the blood and CO2 moves from the blood into the alveolus. (Choice C) is correct: O2 flows down its gradient (100 to 40 mmHg) into the blood and CO2 flows down its gradient (45 to 40 mmHg) out of the blood, which is exactly the directionality set up by blood that arrives oxygen-poor and CO2-rich. (Choice A) is incorrect: gases do not both move in the same direction; CO2 is actually higher in the blood (45 mmHg) than in the alveolus (40 mmHg), so CO2 leaves the blood rather than entering it, and each gas responds only to its own gradient, not to a total 'dissolved gas' content. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses both gradients; O2 is lower in the blood than in the alveolus so O2 enters (not exits) the blood, and CO2 is higher in the blood so CO2 exits (not enters). This exchange oxygenates the blood rather than 'restoring' its incoming composition. (Choice D) is incorrect: the gradients are not negligible. A 60 mmHg difference for O2 and a 5 mmHg difference for CO2 are sufficient to drive net diffusion, which is precisely why oxygen-poor, CO2-rich blood arriving at the alveoli favors exchange. This item tests the skill of reasoning about diffusion direction from partial-pressure data across the respiratory membrane.$px$ WHERE id = 'fb35af2e-1804-4a8a-87a5-acff5d651e27';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because both mutarotation and the ability to reduce \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) depend on the same structural feature: an anomeric carbon that can reopen to the open-chain aldehyde. In free D-glucose, C1 is a cyclic hemiacetal that reversibly opens to an aldehyde; the aldehyde re-closes to either the α or β ring (producing mutarotation) and is also oxidizable (reducing \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) to brick-red \(\text{Cu}_2\text{O}\)). Forming methyl α-D-glucopyranoside converts that hemiacetal into a full acetal (a glycoside): the C1 oxygen is now committed to the methyl bridge, the ring can no longer open, and no aldehyde is ever exposed. With the anomeric carbon locked, the α form cannot equilibrate with β and no open aldehyde exists to be oxidized, so the glycoside neither mutarotates nor reduces Benedict's reagent.

(Choice A) is incorrect: wrong because it grants mutarotation while denying reduction; both properties share the identical requirement of a re-openable anomeric center, so they cannot be separated this way. A locked acetal loses both, not just one.

(Choice B) is incorrect: wrong for the mirror-image reason: it denies mutarotation but claims reduction. Reduction of \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) requires the same free aldehyde that mutarotation needs, so a glycoside that cannot mutarotate also cannot reduce the reagent.

(Choice D) is incorrect: treats the product as if its anomeric carbon were still a free hemiacetal, which describes the starting glucose, not the glycoside; the acetal linkage removes the open-chain aldehyde entirely, so both properties are absent.

(Choice C) is correct: because trapping C1 in a glycosidic (acetal) bond eliminates ring-opening, removing the open-chain aldehyde responsible for both mutarotation and \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) reduction.

Skill 2: rather than recalling a labeled definition, you must connect the reaction described (hemiacetal → acetal) to its structural consequence (no ring-opening) and then deduce that a single change withdraws two seemingly separate properties at once.$px$ WHERE id = 'fb369400-4c71-4729-9403-c87ce925991d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The bone matrix is a calcium-protein composite: an organic framework of collagen fibers on which an inorganic mineral, hydroxyapatite (a form of calcium phosphate), crystallizes. The two components divide the mechanical labor. The hydroxyapatite crystals give bone its hardness and strength, while the collagen fibers give it flexibility so that it is not brittle. This can be understood as a pair of thought experiments: without collagen, bones would be brittle and shatter easily; without mineral crystals, bones would flex and provide little support. (Choice A) is incorrect: increased flexibility and bending are what result when the mineral component is lost, not the collagen. Without mineral crystals bone would flex and provide little support, so this reverses which component was impaired. (Choice B) is correct: collagen fibers are what keep bone from being brittle, so severely reducing collagen while mineral deposition continues leaves an over-mineralized matrix that is hard but brittle and prone to shattering under load. (Choice C) is incorrect: calcium is stored in and released from the mineral (hydroxyapatite) fraction of the matrix, which is deposited normally here, so calcium-storage capacity is retained; the mechanical failure, not a storage failure, is the key consequence of losing collagen. (Choice D) is incorrect: softening and compression into a misshapen form describe under-mineralized bone, as seen when calcium/mineral is lacking (for example rickets); a bone with normal mineral but deficient collagen is not soft but brittle. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the division of labor between the organic (collagen) and inorganic (hydroxyapatite) components of the bone matrix to predict the mechanical outcome of losing one component in a novel scenario.$px$ WHERE id = 'fc28327f-05d8-4fc0-9e5f-99c3a9e3d0be';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized since its refractory period is prolonged by the action-potential plateau and lasts nearly as long as the contraction, leaving no window for a second stimulus to summate. In skeletal muscle, the action potential and its refractory period are extremely brief (the whole action potential takes roughly 2 ms), so a fiber that is still in the relaxation phase of a twitch can be re-stimulated; rapid stimulation makes successive twitches fuse into wave summation and, at high enough frequency, complete tetanus. In cardiac contractile cells the slow \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels produce a long plateau phase, giving an absolute refractory period of about 200 ms and a total refractory period near 250 ms (almost the full duration of the mechanical contraction), so the cell has finished or nearly finished contracting before it can be excited again, which prevents the summation needed for tetany. (Choice A) I only is wrong because statement I is false. (Choice B) III only is wrong because it omits the true statement II. (Choice C) I and III only is wrong because it includes the false statement I and omits the true statement II. (Choice D) is correct: statements II and III are both true and together explain the prohibition on cardiac tetanus. The plateau phase (III) lengthens the absolute refractory period so that the refractory period spans nearly the entire contraction (II), eliminating any interval in which a second action potential could summate; statement I is false because skeletal fibers do have a refractory period, but it is brief (about 2 ms) and ends long before the twitch's mechanical relaxation, which is precisely what allows skeletal summation and tetanus. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between action-potential timing and contraction timing to two different muscle types to explain why one can be tetanized and the other cannot.$px$ WHERE id = 'fc6d4ce3-cb25-4bc6-a01c-e297042e45fd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Citric Acid Cycle.'

The answer is B because the labeled carbons are lost only at oxidative decarboxylation steps, and there are three such steps in this pathway. One glucose yields two pyruvate, and each pyruvate is decarboxylated once by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (pyruvate(3C) \(\rightarrow\) acetyl-CoA(2C) + CO2), giving \(2 \times 1 = 2\) CO2. Each of the two acetyl units then drives one turn of the cycle, and each turn loses 2 CO2 at its two oxidative decarboxylations: isocitrate \(\rightarrow\) \(\alpha\)-ketoglutarate (isocitrate dehydrogenase) and \(\alpha\)-ketoglutarate \(\rightarrow\) succinyl-CoA (\(\alpha\)-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase). Two turns therefore lose \(2 \times 2 = 4\) CO2. Summing, \(2 + 4 = 6\) labeled CO2 per glucose.

(Choice B) is correct: tracing WHERE carbon is lost identifies exactly three decarboxylating steps, one at pyruvate dehydrogenase (occurring twice, once per pyruvate) and two per cycle turn (occurring across two turns). Together they account for \(2 + 4 = 6\) CO2.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it omits the two pyruvate dehydrogenase decarboxylations, which occur before the cycle and each release a CO2. The prompt specifies trapping begins the moment pyruvate is formed, so those 2 CO2 must be counted; the total is 6, not 4.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it wrongly assigns a decarboxylation to malate dehydrogenase. That step (malate \(\rightarrow\) oxaloacetate) is an NAD\(^+\)-linked oxidation that produces the third NADH but releases no CO2. Only isocitrate dehydrogenase and \(\alpha\)-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase decarboxylate within the cycle, so each turn loses 2, not 3, CO2.

(Choice D) is incorrect: although the cycle does regenerate oxaloacetate, it still releases 2 CO2 per turn because the two carbons lost are not the same two that entered as acetyl-CoA on that turn. The cycle is not carbon-neutral per turn. Two turns thus release 4 CO2, which added to the 2 from pyruvate dehydrogenase gives 6.

At the Skill 4 (data-based reasoning) level, the student localizes carbon loss to specific decarboxylating enzymes and scales those steps across the pathway rather than recalling a memorized total.$px$ WHERE id = 'fc9dcb74-b5fb-42d3-b9d7-fb96bdce9741';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because basal metabolic rate rises as body mass increases; the person who gained substantial mass therefore expends more energy at complete rest than the otherwise-identical lighter person.

(Choice D) is correct: greater body mass raises BMR, so the heavier person's resting energy expenditure is higher.
(Choice A) is incorrect: increasing body mass raises, not lowers, the basal metabolic rate.
(Choice B) is incorrect: BMR is not fixed. It tracks body size (as well as lean mass and thyroid hormone), so it is not independent of body mass.
(Choice C) is incorrect: the increased expenditure applies to the resting baseline itself (the BMR), not only to periods of activity.

This item tests Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving (Skill 2): you must reason from a described change in body mass to the resting energy-expenditure consequence rather than recall a fact.$px$ WHERE id = 'fca3f378-7b71-4095-a888-fd09b2728079';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is D because at the completion of meiosis I a daughter cell already contains only one set of chromosomes (it is haploid), but each of those chromosomes is still made of two sister chromatids that do not separate until meiosis II. The text states that "Two haploid cells are the result of the first meiotic division of a diploid cell... This is why the cells are considered haploid: there is only one chromosome set, even though each chromosome still consists of two sister chromatids" (lines 13860-13863), and that "In meiosis II, these two sister chromatids will separate, creating four haploid daughter cells" (lines 13864-13865). Thus the reduction in ploidy is already complete, but the DNA per chromosome has not yet halved, so the cell does not yet have a gamete's final DNA content. (Choice A) is wrong because meiosis reduces the starting diploid nucleus to haploid. The text notes that "the starting nucleus is always diploid and the daughter nuclei that result are haploid" (lines 13673-13674), so the cell is no longer 2n after meiosis I. (Choice B) is wrong because the sister chromatids do not separate during meiosis I; they "will separate" in meiosis II (lines 13864-13865), so each chromosome is still two chromatids, not one, and the DNA content is not yet that of a finished gamete. (Choice C) is wrong because a tetrad, "a total of four sister chromatids of each pair of homologous chromosomes" (lines 13746-13747), is a prophase-I structure held together at chiasmata that "are broken in anaphase I as the microtubules... pull the homologous chromosomes apart" (lines 13847-13848), so no four-chromatid unit survives into telophase I. (Choice D) is correct because the cell is haploid yet each chromosome retains its two sister chromatids until they separate in meiosis II. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires distinguishing ploidy (chromosome-set number) from per-chromosome chromatid/DNA content and predicting the precise structural state of a cell at a defined point in the meiotic sequence.$px$ WHERE id = 'fcf40a19-bab4-4734-b9db-7f86a56f351b';

COMMIT;
